00:21:10,803 --> 00:21:12,339
-Would you look at
the skull on that guy?

514
00:21:12,372 --> 00:21:15,575
-He looks like my dead Uncle
Phil now.

515
00:21:15,608 --> 00:21:17,076
-So if it meets with your
approval, I'll make another...

516
00:21:17,109 --> 00:21:20,246
-I wonder how much
he charges to haunt a house.

517
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:21,914
-You think you can do it?
-I'm not sure,

518
00:21:21,948 --> 00:21:23,115
but it's worth trying.

519
00:21:23,149 --> 00:21:24,351
Anything is better
than sitting here

520
00:21:24,384 --> 00:21:26,018
like tin ducks
in a shooting gallery.

521
00:21:26,052 --> 00:21:27,487
-I heartily agree.

522
00:21:27,520 --> 00:21:29,255
If you're willing to make
the attempt,

523
00:21:29,288 --> 00:21:30,490
you can certainly
have permission.

524
00:21:30,523 --> 00:21:32,325
How soon can you start?
-Right away.

525
00:21:32,359 --> 00:21:34,226
The rocket ship is
all fueled and ready.

526
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:36,228
Joan...
-Rocket ship?

527
00:21:36,262 --> 00:21:38,130
We're going back to the
Moon it sounds like.

528
00:21:38,164 --> 00:21:39,899
-Terrific.
-I want to get started before

529
00:21:39,932 --> 00:21:41,601
there's any chance
of the news leaking out

530
00:21:41,634 --> 00:21:44,303
so Retik won't be laying for us
when we get there.

531
00:21:44,337 --> 00:21:46,606
-Mm.

532
00:21:46,639 --> 00:21:48,074
Watch the fin, everybody.
Whoops.

533
00:21:48,107 --> 00:21:50,176
-Oh. Ooh.

534
00:21:50,209 --> 00:21:52,945
-Scarecrow, I think I'll miss
you most of all.

535
00:21:52,979 --> 00:21:54,781
-Hope this trip is as easy
as your last one.

536
00:21:54,814 --> 00:21:55,982
-Thanks.
It should be.

537
00:21:56,015 --> 00:21:57,083
-Since we're using
the same footage.

538
00:21:57,116 --> 00:21:57,984
-Bye.
-Goodbye.

539
00:21:58,017 --> 00:21:59,819
-Goodbye, sir.
-Bye.

540
00:21:59,852 --> 00:22:01,988
-The last one wasn't easy
at all. They got shot at.

541
00:22:02,021 --> 00:22:03,790
-You know, they should invest
in a control tower.

542
00:22:03,823 --> 00:22:04,891
-Hiya, folks.
Here we go again.

543
00:22:04,924 --> 00:22:06,426
-Hello, Hank.
All set?

544
00:22:06,459 --> 00:22:07,560
-Any time now.

545
00:22:07,594 --> 00:22:15,134
**

546
00:22:15,167 --> 00:22:22,675
**

547
00:22:22,709 --> 00:22:26,746
Safety belts fastened?
-No, it's not a law yet.

548
00:22:26,779 --> 00:22:32,318
-Fire pilot jets.
-Well, one jet.

549
00:22:32,351 --> 00:22:34,754
-Fire main jets!
-Fire all the jets.

550
00:22:34,787 --> 00:22:36,556
Get the Osmonds
back on contract.

551
00:22:36,589 --> 00:22:41,293
**

552
00:22:41,327 --> 00:22:42,895
-This thing is loaded
with candy.

553
00:22:42,929 --> 00:22:44,497
Watch what I can do with it.

554
00:22:44,531 --> 00:22:54,106
**

555
00:22:54,140 --> 00:22:56,242
You know, I could
get used to this.

556
00:22:56,275 --> 00:22:59,612
-Yes, yes.

557
00:22:59,646 --> 00:23:03,450
-Cruising around in a big jet,
lots of beer.

558
00:23:03,483 --> 00:23:05,117
-We're at full speed, Hank.

559
00:23:05,151 --> 00:23:06,419
Set a course for the Moon.

560
00:23:06,453 --> 00:23:08,320
-Which moon?
-Right.

561
00:23:08,354 --> 00:23:10,823
-Hey, take a left at L2.
You can't miss it.

562
00:23:10,857 --> 00:23:14,761
Dave's van is parked out front.

563
00:23:14,794 --> 00:23:16,162
-Let me show you
what this baby can do.

564
00:23:16,195 --> 00:23:17,997
You guys ever seen
a barrel roll?

565
00:23:18,030 --> 00:23:20,299
[Chuckles]
-Yikes.

566
00:23:20,332 --> 00:23:30,309
**

567
00:23:30,342 --> 00:23:33,312
-Yahtzee.

568
00:23:33,345 --> 00:23:34,947
-If I'd have brought that
straight edge,

569
00:23:34,981 --> 00:23:36,983
we'd make it to the Moon
in half the time.

570
00:23:37,016 --> 00:23:38,685
-Here, use my forehead, Phil.

571
00:23:38,718 --> 00:23:42,555
**

572
00:23:42,589 --> 00:23:43,623
-We're just about here.

573
00:23:48,294 --> 00:23:50,162
We ought to be down
in another 2 hours.

574
00:23:50,196 --> 00:23:55,968
**

575
00:23:56,002 --> 00:23:57,069
-I'm getting really hungry.

576
00:23:57,103 --> 00:24:00,673
Everyone, look
for the Stuckey's.

577
00:24:00,707 --> 00:24:02,642
-I can see the city now.

578
00:24:02,675 --> 00:24:04,243
We don't want to get too close,
so swing west,

579
00:24:04,276 --> 00:24:05,812
Hank, and head
for that same canyon.

580
00:24:05,845 --> 00:24:07,346
-Okay.

581
00:24:07,379 --> 00:24:13,452
**

582
00:24:13,486 --> 00:24:15,488
-I see 1,000 points of light.

583
00:24:15,522 --> 00:24:16,589
It looks like Cleveland.

584
00:24:16,623 --> 00:24:17,890
-Shh.
-Okay. I'll take her down.

585
00:24:17,924 --> 00:24:22,729
**

586
00:24:22,762 --> 00:24:24,463
-We are now
approaching the Moon.

587
00:24:24,497 --> 00:24:27,099
Please place your office chairs
into the upright position.

588
00:24:27,133 --> 00:24:29,769
The temperature on the Moon
is a balmy 70 degrees.

589
00:24:29,802 --> 00:24:31,170
Enjoy your stay.

590
00:24:31,203 --> 00:24:33,973
-All right.
Set 'er down.

591
00:24:34,006 --> 00:24:35,808
-Kind of looks like
the Upper Dells.

592
00:24:35,842 --> 00:24:39,178
-Yeah.

593
00:24:39,211 --> 00:24:41,380
-Wisconsin's a beautiful
place to be.

594
00:24:44,717 --> 00:24:45,985
-You sure you don't want me
to go along?

595
00:24:46,018 --> 00:24:47,520
-No, not this time.

596
00:24:47,554 --> 00:24:48,755
This is just a scouting trip

597
00:24:48,788 --> 00:24:50,489
to try to find
where they keep the Lunarium.

598
00:24:50,523 --> 00:24:52,324
-How do you expect to do that?
-Well, if I'm lucky,

599
00:24:52,358 --> 00:24:53,726
I'll be able to capture
one of the Moon men

600
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:55,662
and get some information
out of him.

601
00:24:55,695 --> 00:24:57,263
-Well, bundle up, honey.

602
00:24:57,296 --> 00:24:58,598
It's cold out there.

603
00:24:58,631 --> 00:25:08,507
**

604
00:25:08,541 --> 00:25:09,809
-Ugh, radishes.

605
00:25:09,842 --> 00:25:10,977
[Clears throat]

606
00:25:11,010 --> 00:25:17,750
**

607
00:25:17,784 --> 00:25:20,252
-Good morning.
Where's my paper?

608
00:25:20,286 --> 00:25:22,254
Where's our shrubs?
Oh, I'm on the Moon.

609
00:25:22,288 --> 00:25:24,657
How silly of me.

610
00:25:24,691 --> 00:25:27,159
Um, space travel.

611
00:25:27,193 --> 00:25:28,427
-And away!

612
00:25:28,460 --> 00:25:32,131
**

613
00:25:32,164 --> 00:25:33,966
-So sunny on the Moon today.

614
00:25:36,468 --> 00:25:38,004
-Huh?

615
00:25:38,037 --> 00:25:41,240
-Oh, I'm just standing on
this hill, no lookin' around.

616
00:25:41,273 --> 00:25:43,876
Oh, there I am.
-It's Don Knotts.

617
00:25:47,013 --> 00:25:48,948
-Huh?
-Give me that gun.

618
00:25:48,981 --> 00:25:50,349
It's mine.

619
00:25:50,382 --> 00:25:58,424
**

620
00:25:58,457 --> 00:26:00,993
-Oh, it's that old
"Sea Hunt" routine.

621
00:26:01,027 --> 00:26:08,567
**

622
00:26:08,601 --> 00:26:09,902
-Pure helium?

623
00:26:09,936 --> 00:26:11,671
What are you tryin' to do,
kill me?

624
00:26:14,473 --> 00:26:16,108
-Do you want me to cut
off your air again?

625
00:26:16,142 --> 00:26:17,276
-No.

626
00:26:17,309 --> 00:26:19,011
-Uh-uh.
-No.

627
00:26:19,045 --> 00:26:23,650
-Where is the Lunarium stored?
-I will tell you.

628
00:26:23,683 --> 00:26:25,752
-Think I'll pass?
-Sure, you'll pass all right.

629
00:26:25,785 --> 00:26:27,486
-Look. They're forcing him
to eat Joan's cooking.

630
00:26:27,519 --> 00:26:28,888
-He wasn't in any position
to lie very well.

631
00:26:28,921 --> 00:26:30,289
-He's just getting his free
continental breakfast.

632
00:26:30,322 --> 00:26:31,658
-I was just lucky I picked
on one of the men

633
00:26:31,691 --> 00:26:32,725
who guard the Lunarium.
-I can't believe

634
00:26:32,759 --> 00:26:33,926
we're trying
to annihilate you.

635
00:26:33,960 --> 00:26:35,094
This is delicious!

636
00:26:35,127 --> 00:26:36,729
Uh, hey, pass the jam, will you?

637
00:26:36,763 --> 00:26:43,502
**

638
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:45,571
-It's a little after 9:00.
I'll go right in.

639
00:26:45,604 --> 00:26:48,007
You find a place to hide
and watch for me to come out.

640
00:26:48,040 --> 00:26:49,909
-Okay.
-Geez, he got right through

641
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:50,977
their rigid security system.

642
00:26:51,010 --> 00:26:52,611
I can't believe that.

643
00:26:52,645 --> 00:26:58,751
**

644
00:26:58,785 --> 00:27:04,957
**

645
00:27:04,991 --> 00:27:06,726
-About time you got here.

646
00:27:06,759 --> 00:27:07,994
-I was delayed.

647
00:27:09,996 --> 00:27:11,497
-I'm a phony, you know.

648
00:27:11,530 --> 00:27:12,899
[Laughs]

649
00:27:12,932 --> 00:27:20,239
**

650
00:27:20,272 --> 00:27:22,742
-Oh, that's right.
The Sonic closes after 10:00.

651
00:27:22,775 --> 00:27:25,477
Well, I'll have to use
my after-hours pass key.

652
00:27:25,511 --> 00:27:31,350
**

653
00:27:31,383 --> 00:27:37,223
**

654
00:27:37,256 --> 00:27:40,259
-All right.
I found my Nintendo.

655
00:27:40,292 --> 00:27:42,661
I'm gonna play "Super Mario
Bros." and everything.

656
00:27:42,695 --> 00:27:43,662
This is gonna be so great.

657
00:27:43,696 --> 00:27:45,331
Oh, it's heavy.

658
00:27:45,364 --> 00:27:50,402
**

659
00:27:50,436 --> 00:27:53,973
**

660
00:27:54,006 --> 00:27:55,942
[Air releasing]

661
00:27:55,975 --> 00:27:57,476
-Oh, I'm not sorry
for that one.

662
00:27:57,509 --> 00:27:59,011
That felt wonderful.

663
00:27:59,045 --> 00:28:00,579
[Coughing]

664
00:28:00,612 --> 00:28:02,681
Oh, I shouldn't do that
in a pressure suit.

665
00:28:02,715 --> 00:28:04,183
[Coughs] Ugh.

666
00:28:04,216 --> 00:28:06,786
**

667
00:28:06,819 --> 00:28:09,421
-You got it.
-Yes, it's so heavy,

668
00:28:09,455 --> 00:28:11,290
it's going to take us a week
to lug it back to the ship.

669
00:28:11,323 --> 00:28:12,658
-[Laughs]

670
00:28:12,691 --> 00:28:14,160
-Get out of sight, quick.

671
00:28:14,193 --> 00:28:22,001
**

672
00:28:22,034 --> 00:28:24,804
-You know, these Moon men
have uses for plywood

673
00:28:24,837 --> 00:28:26,605
we haven't even dreamed of yet.

674
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:30,977
-You wait in the car.

675
00:28:31,010 --> 00:28:32,611
I'm gonna go see
if Judy's ready.

676
00:28:32,644 --> 00:28:41,253
**

677
00:28:41,287 --> 00:28:42,588
-Come on.

678
00:28:42,621 --> 00:28:46,425
**

679
00:28:46,458 --> 00:28:49,395
-Judy, I'm down in the lobby.
Can you buzz me up?

680
00:28:49,428 --> 00:28:51,097
Got Dad's car.

681
00:28:51,130 --> 00:28:57,269
**

682
00:28:57,303 --> 00:28:59,806
A case of Moosehead, no curfew.

683
00:28:59,839 --> 00:29:01,908
This is gonna be
the best road trip ever.

684
00:29:01,941 --> 00:29:03,910
-Yeah.

685
00:29:03,943 --> 00:29:05,477
-Here's a lesson
for you teens --

686
00:29:05,511 --> 00:29:07,479
Always lock up
and take the keys.

687
00:29:07,513 --> 00:29:09,448
Don't let a good boy go bad.

688
00:29:09,481 --> 00:29:10,749
-Very well.

689
00:29:10,783 --> 00:29:17,456
**

690
00:29:17,489 --> 00:29:20,392
-Oh, Dad's gonna be torqued.

691
00:29:20,426 --> 00:29:21,828
I got to make up a story.

692
00:29:21,861 --> 00:29:23,595
Hmm.
Wandered to the far side...

693
00:29:23,629 --> 00:29:25,431
Yeah, that's it.
-Somebody stole my car.

694
00:29:25,464 --> 00:29:28,968
Send another one quickly.
-Listen.

695
00:29:29,001 --> 00:29:30,436
I'm not gonna bail you
out of trouble

696
00:29:30,469 --> 00:29:31,770
every time you lose a car.

697
00:29:31,804 --> 00:29:34,173
Someday, I'm not gonna
be there for you.

698
00:29:34,206 --> 00:29:36,775
-Yeah! Whoo-hoo!
-How's the fuel pressure?

699
00:29:36,809 --> 00:29:39,011
-Seems okay.

700
00:29:42,548 --> 00:29:44,450
-This is so great.

701
00:29:47,219 --> 00:29:49,488
-What those Duke boys
didn't know is that

702
00:29:49,521 --> 00:29:52,324
Boss Hogg
had stolen the General Lee.

703
00:29:52,358 --> 00:29:54,726
[Vocalizes "Dixie"]

704
00:29:54,760 --> 00:29:56,795
Yee-haw!
Yah yah!

705
00:29:56,829 --> 00:29:58,397
Whoo!

706
00:29:58,430 --> 00:30:08,407
**

707
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:11,543
-Take all the speed you can.
-There he is.

708
00:30:13,946 --> 00:30:16,115
-Let's try for a near miss.
That might stop him.

709
00:30:18,817 --> 00:30:20,119
-Whoa.

710
00:30:20,152 --> 00:30:22,588
-What was that?
-A ray gun blast.

711
00:30:22,621 --> 00:30:24,256
See what's behind us.

712
00:30:24,290 --> 00:30:25,992
-Uh, I think it's a ray gun.

713
00:30:26,025 --> 00:30:29,828
**

714
00:30:29,862 --> 00:30:31,063
-Oh, it's just some car.

715
00:30:31,097 --> 00:30:32,498
That must mean they want
to pass us.

716
00:30:32,531 --> 00:30:33,900
-Huh?
-Mood signal or something.

717
00:30:33,933 --> 00:30:36,635
-There's another car chasing us!
We'd better use our gun.

718
00:30:36,668 --> 00:30:38,804
-I have to turn around to do it.

719
00:30:38,837 --> 00:30:40,439
There's a bunch of rocks ahead
that will give us some shelter.

720
00:30:40,472 --> 00:30:42,108
We'll wait until we get there.

721
00:30:42,141 --> 00:30:47,914
**

722
00:30:47,947 --> 00:30:49,949
-He's heading for some rocks!
We'll have to stop him.

723
00:30:49,982 --> 00:30:55,287
**

724
00:30:55,321 --> 00:30:58,057
-Oh, it's the "Das Boot"
theme all over again.

725
00:30:58,090 --> 00:30:59,625
-"Das Moon Boot."
-...pressure line.

726
00:30:59,658 --> 00:31:02,061
We're stuck.
-I'll say we are.

727
00:31:02,094 --> 00:31:03,963
-The pressure's down
to nothing.

728
00:31:03,996 --> 00:31:05,164
I could fix this if I have time.

729
00:31:05,197 --> 00:31:08,300
-I took moon mechanics
in high school.

730
00:31:08,334 --> 00:31:10,769
-Okay.

731
00:31:10,802 --> 00:31:14,173
-We're stuck behind a rock,
but I think I can blast it.

732
00:31:14,206 --> 00:31:22,514
**

733
00:31:22,548 --> 00:31:26,685
-By this time, my lungs
were aching for air.

734
00:31:26,718 --> 00:31:29,121
-You hit the helium!

735
00:31:29,155 --> 00:31:31,057
-[High pitched]
Help me! Help me!

736
00:31:31,090 --> 00:31:32,058
-We got to get out of here,
you guys.

737
00:31:32,091 --> 00:31:33,659
-Help me! Oh, no!

738
00:31:33,692 --> 00:31:40,899
**

739
00:31:40,933 --> 00:31:42,634
[Doors thud and creak]

740
00:31:42,668 --> 00:31:45,671
[Doors whir]

741
00:31:45,704 --> 00:31:48,074
[Doors thud and clank]

742
00:31:48,107 --> 00:31:50,476
[Door slams]

743
00:31:50,509 --> 00:31:52,244
-Okay, Ted. Stop here.
I'm gonna hover here

744
00:31:52,278 --> 00:31:53,912
for a moment and survey
the landscape.

745
00:31:53,946 --> 00:31:55,614
-[Imitating rocket engines]

746
00:31:55,647 --> 00:31:57,649
-Okay. Now I think I'm gonna
fly over to that window

747
00:31:57,683 --> 00:31:59,986
and look for those
rotten radar men from the Moon.

748
00:32:00,019 --> 00:32:02,188
-Are you done yet?
-No, I'm not done yet!

749
00:32:02,221 --> 00:32:03,990
Okay. Let's do a fly-by
of that nudist colony.

750
00:32:04,023 --> 00:32:05,124
Swing it into high, Teddy!

751
00:32:05,157 --> 00:32:07,326
I feel the need,
the need for speed!

752
00:32:07,359 --> 00:32:09,428
-Come on, Servo.
-Who?

753
00:32:09,461 --> 00:32:11,230
-I mean, Commando Servo.

754
00:32:11,263 --> 00:32:13,565
I think Crog wants
to talk to us.

755
00:32:13,599 --> 00:32:14,733
-You're a good
little helper, Ted.

756
00:32:14,766 --> 00:32:16,302
Let's go! Come on.

757
00:32:16,335 --> 00:32:18,004
-Hey, you guy --
I mean...[clears throat]

758
00:32:18,037 --> 00:32:19,105
Earth men.

759
00:32:19,138 --> 00:32:21,107
I want you to take
an atomic bomb

760
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:24,343
the size of a pineapple,
strap it to a piper cob,

761
00:32:24,376 --> 00:32:26,979
and then crash into
Mount Vesuvius.

762
00:32:27,013 --> 00:32:29,515
Then, on your way back,
swing by Al's and pick me up

763
00:32:29,548 --> 00:32:31,750
a, uh, Swiss cheese
on a Kaiser roll.

764
00:32:31,783 --> 00:32:34,086
Oh, I can't get into this.

765
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:36,755
Joel, I want to be
Commander Cody for a while.

766
00:32:36,788 --> 00:32:39,591
-Ted, who is that squeaky little
worm in the kaftan down there?

767
00:32:39,625 --> 00:32:42,228
-It's Crog, and he wants
to wear your costume.

768
00:32:42,261 --> 00:32:44,063
-Doesn't he have any idea
who he's dealing with?

769
00:32:44,096 --> 00:32:45,731
I could...
-Eh, you're Servo,

770
00:32:45,764 --> 00:32:46,932
and Joel's holdin' you up.

771
00:32:46,965 --> 00:32:48,767
-Pay no attention to the man
holding me up.

772
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:50,336
Take me down, Ted.
I'm on a roll.

773
00:32:50,369 --> 00:32:52,571
-Oh, come on, Servo.
Don't be such a baby.

774
00:32:52,604 --> 00:32:54,106
-All right, Crog.
The jig is up!

775
00:32:54,140 --> 00:32:56,175
Put down that cheese pistol
and fight me like a man.

776
00:32:56,208 --> 00:32:58,010
-Oh, jam it in your venturi,
bubblehead!

777
00:32:58,044 --> 00:32:59,078
Come on!
-Let's go, moon man!

778
00:32:59,111 --> 00:32:59,945
-Give me that -- Hey!
-Whoa!

779
00:32:59,978 --> 00:33:01,747
-Saved by commercial sign!

780
00:33:01,780 --> 00:33:03,615
-Oh, you're lucky.
My check's here.

781
00:33:03,649 --> 00:33:08,020
**

782
00:33:10,856 --> 00:33:15,894
**

783
00:33:15,927 --> 00:33:17,596
-Fly me in, Ted.
-Nope.

784
00:33:17,629 --> 00:33:21,400
Next time, Crow gets to be,
uh, C-Commando Cody.

785
00:33:21,433 --> 00:33:23,102
-You hear that?
He said I could do it.

786
00:33:23,135 --> 00:33:26,605
-Commando Crow will never work.

787
00:33:26,638 --> 00:33:29,841
You're too much of a pansy.

788
00:33:29,875 --> 00:33:32,578
-That's it. That's it.

789
00:33:32,611 --> 00:33:35,247
**

790
00:33:35,281 --> 00:33:37,015
I don't know what it is,
but it's it.

791
00:33:37,049 --> 00:33:38,617
-Robert Heinlein?

792
00:33:38,650 --> 00:33:43,455
**

793
00:33:43,489 --> 00:33:47,159
-Hey, get me, fellows!
I'm playin' the violin.

794
00:33:47,193 --> 00:33:50,929
-Jerome Pycha Jr.,
cousin of Jerome Elite!

795
00:33:50,962 --> 00:33:53,465
[Laughs]
Uh, kind of a typist joke.

796
00:33:55,667 --> 00:33:59,238
-Miniatures by Jacque.
What are the miniatures for?

797
00:33:59,271 --> 00:34:01,340
-Uh, they used a lot
of Hummel figurines

798
00:34:01,373 --> 00:34:03,475
in the, uh, space sequences.
-Oh.

799
00:34:03,509 --> 00:34:11,083
**

800
00:34:11,117 --> 00:34:18,657
**

801
00:34:18,690 --> 00:34:20,992
-Talmadge Farm remembers!

802
00:34:21,026 --> 00:34:23,729
**

803
00:34:23,762 --> 00:34:26,132
-In 1948, the Secretary
of Defense

804
00:34:26,165 --> 00:34:28,167
proposed that the United States
build a Space Station

805
00:34:28,200 --> 00:34:30,469
as a military guardian
of the sky.

806
00:34:30,502 --> 00:34:33,439
By 1954 atom bombs
and inter-continental rockets

807
00:34:33,472 --> 00:34:34,773
made it a necessity.

808
00:34:34,806 --> 00:34:38,244
In 1963, The Beatles
first appeared on "Ed Sullivan."

809
00:34:38,277 --> 00:34:40,679
In 1966, the first orbital
flight was made

810
00:34:40,712 --> 00:34:42,314
by Colonel Briteis.

811
00:34:42,348 --> 00:34:44,283
By 1970, the Space Station
had been built,

812
00:34:44,316 --> 00:34:45,917
and free men
were reaching the Moon.

813
00:34:45,951 --> 00:34:50,756
1977, hot pants became
the height of fashion.

814
00:34:50,789 --> 00:34:52,291
While this was going on,
the enemies of Freedom

815
00:34:52,324 --> 00:34:53,592
were not idle.

816
00:34:53,625 --> 00:34:57,463
They were working to destroy
the Space Station.

817
00:34:57,496 --> 00:34:59,231
-Sector number 12,
come in, please.

818
00:34:59,265 --> 00:35:01,333
Sector number 12.
-Inspector 12?

819
00:35:01,367 --> 00:35:03,735
He checked my underwear!
-Hey, who hasn't?

820
00:35:03,769 --> 00:35:05,771
-Sector number 13,
come in, please.

821
00:35:05,804 --> 00:35:09,007
-Sector number 13.
-Just doing laundry.

822
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:11,143
-Sector number 13,
come in, please!

823
00:35:11,177 --> 00:35:13,679
Sector number 13,
come in, please!

824
00:35:13,712 --> 00:35:16,148
-Sector number 13, in.

825
00:35:16,182 --> 00:35:19,485
-We want to
recall your underwear.

826
00:35:19,518 --> 00:35:21,653
-Mr. Roundtree, we're all ready
for you, sir.

827
00:35:21,687 --> 00:35:23,722
-Entree, Roundtree.

828
00:35:23,755 --> 00:35:25,657
Hey, it's Bob Newhart!

829
00:35:25,691 --> 00:35:27,359
-No, it's Bob Hope.

830
00:35:29,595 --> 00:35:31,129
-It's Toody and Muldoon!

831
00:35:31,163 --> 00:35:33,599
Ooh, ooh!

832
00:35:33,632 --> 00:35:37,336
-He's gonna get fired!

833
00:35:37,369 --> 00:35:39,971
-Look at that, the fondue
set of the future.

834
00:35:40,005 --> 00:35:41,907
-Ha-ha-ha-ha.
Look at the little tie.

835
00:35:41,940 --> 00:35:43,975
They must have got
into a tie-cutting gag.

836
00:35:44,009 --> 00:35:45,911
-Roundtree, here.
-Joel, here.

837
00:35:45,944 --> 00:35:47,313
-Servo, here.
-Crow, here.

838
00:35:47,346 --> 00:35:48,814
-Joel, here.
-Servo, here.

839
00:35:48,847 --> 00:35:50,616
-Crow, here.
-I've called you

840
00:35:50,649 --> 00:35:54,620
15 sectional chiefs
to this radio rendezvous

841
00:35:54,653 --> 00:35:58,324
to brief you on the situation
regarding Project "A."

842
00:35:58,357 --> 00:36:03,762
We have tried for 2 years to get
an agent on that space station,

843
00:36:03,795 --> 00:36:07,966
but due to impregnable
security precautions,

844
00:36:07,999 --> 00:36:09,635
we have been unable to do so.

845
00:36:09,668 --> 00:36:12,804
-Sorry I blew up like that.

846
00:36:12,838 --> 00:36:16,174
-I have orders from
the minister himself

847
00:36:16,208 --> 00:36:18,277
that we must not fail
in destroying

848
00:36:18,310 --> 00:36:21,513
this perpetually
menacing eye in the sky,

849
00:36:21,547 --> 00:36:23,915
and it must be done
within 2 months.

850
00:36:23,949 --> 00:36:25,917
-Or I'll be really steamed.

851
00:36:25,951 --> 00:36:27,419
-Therefore, I must have
information

852
00:36:27,453 --> 00:36:30,322
on every person who might be
sent to that space station.

853
00:36:30,356 --> 00:36:33,959
-I have 300 operatives
who are typed to resemble

854
00:36:33,992 --> 00:36:35,827
all leading scientists
in the field.

855
00:36:35,861 --> 00:36:38,163
-And you know
how expensive that is.

856
00:36:38,196 --> 00:36:44,069
-Due to money or family
or some other reason,

857
00:36:44,102 --> 00:36:47,005
these people are under
our absolute control.

858
00:36:47,038 --> 00:36:49,007
Your job will be
to use every man.

859
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:51,610
-Not us.
-...under absolute surveillance.

860
00:36:51,643 --> 00:36:53,845
And, so far, it's possible

861
00:36:53,879 --> 00:36:56,348
to monitor
everything they say and do.

862
00:36:56,382 --> 00:36:59,751
-Except the stupid stuff.
You know, the knock-knock jokes.

863
00:36:59,785 --> 00:37:01,987
-My assistant will now transmit
you the names and addresses

864
00:37:02,020 --> 00:37:03,722
of those people
we wish followed.

865
00:37:03,755 --> 00:37:07,726
-Anderson, Andy, Adams,
Al, Ann, Adam.

866
00:37:07,759 --> 00:37:10,061
-Make this a 24-hour job.

867
00:37:10,095 --> 00:37:12,163
-But don't kill yourself
getting it done.

868
00:37:12,197 --> 00:37:13,632
It's not worth that.

869
00:37:17,969 --> 00:37:19,771
-Oh, the seat's warm! Ick!

870
00:37:19,805 --> 00:37:21,407
-Calling the San
Francisco sector.

871
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:23,275
-Yeah?
-Here is a special list

872
00:37:23,309 --> 00:37:25,411
for your attention.

873
00:37:25,444 --> 00:37:28,246
John Adams, Henry Burns...

874
00:37:28,280 --> 00:37:29,848
[Telephone rings]

875
00:37:29,881 --> 00:37:31,016
Stand by.

876
00:37:33,184 --> 00:37:34,520
Yes?

877
00:37:34,553 --> 00:37:36,121
-Wow.
How futuristic!

878
00:37:38,690 --> 00:37:40,959
-Oh, that phone bill's
gonna be enormous!

879
00:37:40,992 --> 00:37:43,295
-We have information on
the next man to make the trip.

880
00:37:43,329 --> 00:37:44,730
-You have?
When did you find this out?

881
00:37:44,763 --> 00:37:46,898
Just a second.
I'll call Mr. Roundtree.

882
00:37:46,932 --> 00:37:49,000
-Roundtree?
Hey, that's Shaft!

883
00:37:49,034 --> 00:37:51,002
-Important call for you, sir.

884
00:37:51,036 --> 00:37:52,904
-Oh, I was just gnawing
on my own knee.

885
00:37:52,938 --> 00:37:53,939
Excuse me.
[Chuckles]

886
00:37:53,972 --> 00:38:02,748
**

887
00:38:02,781 --> 00:38:04,783
Thank you for warming
up the seat.

888
00:38:04,816 --> 00:38:06,785
-Roundtree, here.

889
00:38:06,818 --> 00:38:09,020
-Your man for Project "A"
is Dr. Wernher.

890
00:38:09,054 --> 00:38:11,590
Repeat, Dr. Wernher.

891
00:38:11,623 --> 00:38:15,761
W-E-R-N-H-E-R.

892
00:38:15,794 --> 00:38:17,429
-What's it spell?
-Wernher!

893
00:38:17,463 --> 00:38:20,399
-What's it mean?
-Diddley!

894
00:38:20,432 --> 00:38:22,000
-Check the files.

895
00:38:22,033 --> 00:38:24,302
See if we have a Dr. Wernher.

896
00:38:24,336 --> 00:38:26,271
W-E-R-N-H-E-R.

897
00:38:26,304 --> 00:38:28,106
-What's that spell?
-Wernher!

898
00:38:28,139 --> 00:38:30,108
-What's it mean?
-Diddley!

899
00:38:30,141 --> 00:38:33,745
-Quickly!

900
00:38:33,779 --> 00:38:34,980
-Well, here you are, sir!

901
00:38:35,013 --> 00:38:36,348
-We have a double for him,
all right.

902
00:38:36,382 --> 00:38:37,783
-Mm-hmm.
-He's in San Francisco

903
00:38:37,816 --> 00:38:40,251
under section chief three.

904
00:38:40,285 --> 00:38:43,389
Exact resemblance,
except for the hair.

905
00:38:43,422 --> 00:38:47,993
-It's the Warner Brothers!
-Ah, that's easy to fix.

906
00:38:48,026 --> 00:38:50,829
-Send me some Grecian Formula.

907
00:38:50,862 --> 00:38:53,599
-Now here's your plan of action.

908
00:38:53,632 --> 00:38:57,769
Dr. Wernher is staying at
the Central Hotel in room 524.

909
00:38:57,803 --> 00:39:00,338
I'll have a set of photographs
to you within 15 minutes.

910
00:39:00,372 --> 00:39:01,673
-It's the future.
We can do that.

911
00:39:01,707 --> 00:39:04,142
-Surround the hotel
with a cordon of operatives.

912
00:39:04,175 --> 00:39:07,112
Get a room as close to him as
possible, preferably next door.

913
00:39:07,145 --> 00:39:10,315
-That's as close as possible.
-I will be there within 2 hours

914
00:39:10,348 --> 00:39:11,983
to take charge
of the operation myself.

915
00:39:13,318 --> 00:39:17,656
**

916
00:39:24,930 --> 00:39:27,999
-They'll never notice this bug
the size of an Epilady.

917
00:39:35,507 --> 00:39:37,008
-Ready, here.
Testing.

918
00:39:37,042 --> 00:39:39,745
1, 2, 3, 4.

919
00:39:39,778 --> 00:39:40,979
-All right. It's in.

920
00:39:41,012 --> 00:39:42,180
We'll be able to hear
everything that goes on.

921
00:39:42,213 --> 00:39:44,149
-I can hear you right
through the door.

922
00:39:46,384 --> 00:39:49,621
-You keep your ear glued to that
receiver until your leave.

923
00:39:49,655 --> 00:39:52,123
-Uh, could you send some solvent
at the end of my shift?

924
00:39:52,157 --> 00:39:53,692
-Say!

925
00:39:56,361 --> 00:39:58,597
-Mr. Roundtree, Dr. Wernher
just came in.

926
00:39:58,630 --> 00:40:00,566
[Telephone rings]

927
00:40:00,599 --> 00:40:01,833
-Hello?

928
00:40:01,867 --> 00:40:03,401
-This is General Greene
speaking.

929
00:40:03,435 --> 00:40:07,005
-Well, this is Colonel Mustard.
-Oh, yes, General.

930
00:40:07,038 --> 00:40:08,940
Delighted to hear from you!

931
00:40:08,974 --> 00:40:10,676
I expected that
you would call me.

932
00:40:10,709 --> 00:40:12,478
Yes.

933
00:40:12,511 --> 00:40:13,912
All arrangements
have been completed

934
00:40:13,945 --> 00:40:15,947
for the circumlunar flight.

935
00:40:15,981 --> 00:40:17,983
-In the future, all phones
will be invisible.

936
00:40:18,016 --> 00:40:19,785
-Yes.
-His hand is glued to his ear.

937
00:40:19,818 --> 00:40:21,119
-I have all my preparations
complete.

938
00:40:21,152 --> 00:40:23,088
-He's a hell of a mime, though.
-Yes, sir.

939
00:40:23,121 --> 00:40:25,624
Mm-hmm.
You'll make arrangements

940
00:40:25,657 --> 00:40:27,225
through the local
transportation office.

941
00:40:27,258 --> 00:40:29,060
Good! Fine.

942
00:40:29,094 --> 00:40:30,261
Thank you.
-Super. Great.

943
00:40:30,295 --> 00:40:31,463
-Hope to see you soon, sir.
-Unbelievable.

944
00:40:31,497 --> 00:40:32,831
Couldn't be better.
Loving life,

945
00:40:32,864 --> 00:40:34,800
but, you know,
before I go, I got -- Oh.

946
00:40:34,833 --> 00:40:37,068
-Where is that double?
-He's shooting a Doublemint ad.

947
00:40:37,102 --> 00:40:37,936
-Wernher might be
leaving any moment.

948
00:40:37,969 --> 00:40:39,437
-He'll be here shortly.

949
00:40:39,471 --> 00:40:41,973
His plane arrived at the airport
25 minutes ago.

950
00:40:42,007 --> 00:40:43,642
-Sir, the glue has set.
[Knock on door]

951
00:40:43,675 --> 00:40:45,043
-Land Shark double.

952
00:40:48,379 --> 00:40:50,115
-Finally!
-Sorry. I was detained.

953
00:40:50,148 --> 00:40:51,483
-I was doubling
for someone else.

954
00:40:51,517 --> 00:40:52,918
-Please be seated.
-It was double duty.

955
00:40:52,951 --> 00:40:54,052
-Thank you.

956
00:40:56,822 --> 00:40:58,089
-You know your job?

957
00:40:58,123 --> 00:40:59,390
-I know.

958
00:40:59,424 --> 00:41:02,661
-You must destroy
the space station.

959
00:41:02,694 --> 00:41:04,329
According to all
my available information,

960
00:41:04,362 --> 00:41:06,064
there is only two ways
of doing it.

961
00:41:06,097 --> 00:41:07,432
-Neither are very good.

962
00:41:07,465 --> 00:41:09,501
-One, by getting into
the bomb room of the station

963
00:41:09,535 --> 00:41:12,403
and setting off
one of the bombs.

964
00:41:12,437 --> 00:41:15,440
Two, by taking control
of the moonship,

965
00:41:15,473 --> 00:41:19,978
which is scheduled to take,
and ramming it into the station.

966
00:41:20,011 --> 00:41:20,979
-There's a bonus
if you get killed.

967
00:41:21,012 --> 00:41:24,115
-You understand?
-I understand.

968
00:41:24,149 --> 00:41:25,517
-Sam, take him into
the dressing room

969
00:41:25,551 --> 00:41:27,152
and match his hair
to that photograph.

970
00:41:27,185 --> 00:41:28,754
-Yes.
-Or match the photo to the hair,

971
00:41:28,787 --> 00:41:30,321
either one.

972
00:41:35,093 --> 00:41:37,729
-Well, how does the hair look?
-Like the photograph!

973
00:41:37,763 --> 00:41:39,898
[Telephone rings]
-Shh!

974
00:41:39,931 --> 00:41:41,767
-Yes?
This is Dr. Wernher.

975
00:41:41,800 --> 00:41:44,269
Why, yes, Colonel.
I've been waiting for you!

976
00:41:44,302 --> 00:41:46,304
A car in front of the hotel
in 10 minutes.

977
00:41:46,337 --> 00:41:49,107
Good. I'll be there, sir.
-A big official car.

978
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:50,776
-An official car!

979
00:41:50,809 --> 00:41:52,744
I'll watch for the insignia.

980
00:41:52,778 --> 00:41:53,612
Thank you, sir.

981
00:41:53,645 --> 00:41:55,747
Goodbye.

982
00:41:55,781 --> 00:41:57,683
-He's gonna leave
in 10 minutes.

983
00:41:57,716 --> 00:42:00,285
-Good.

984
00:42:00,318 --> 00:42:01,587
-Uh, desk?

985
00:42:01,620 --> 00:42:03,354
Uh, Wernher, here.

986
00:42:03,388 --> 00:42:05,156
Uh, send a man up
for my bags, please.

987
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:07,325
Thank you.

988
00:42:07,358 --> 00:42:09,127
-Sent for a bellhop.
-What luck.

989
00:42:09,160 --> 00:42:10,929
I happen to be
dressed as a bellhop.

990
00:42:10,962 --> 00:42:16,835
**

991
00:42:16,868 --> 00:42:18,303
[Knock on door]

992
00:42:18,336 --> 00:42:20,939
-Uh, gorilla bellboy double.
-Come in.

993
00:42:23,441 --> 00:42:25,611
-Oh, you.

994
00:42:25,644 --> 00:42:29,014
Take these, uh, two bags here,
and I'll take a briefcase.

995
00:42:29,047 --> 00:42:32,417
-Uh-oh.
He forgot to say please!

996
00:42:32,450 --> 00:42:35,153
-How did you get here
to quickly?

997
00:42:35,186 --> 00:42:37,623
-That's how I got here
so quickly.

998
00:42:40,158 --> 00:42:41,960
-Now I'm gonna use his Epilady.

999
00:42:45,997 --> 00:42:47,498
-Wow. Look at all this neat
stuff he's got!

1000
00:42:47,532 --> 00:42:49,400
Wow. Great.

1001
00:42:49,434 --> 00:42:50,802
Bet he got this
at the Sharper Image.

1002
00:42:50,836 --> 00:42:52,370
Hey, I'm keeping that.

1003
00:42:55,373 --> 00:42:58,243
-Well, do I look like him?
-If you don't,

1004
00:42:58,276 --> 00:43:00,345
we wasted a full year looking
for you for this assignment.

1005
00:43:00,378 --> 00:43:01,747
-I know that, in your own way,

1006
00:43:01,780 --> 00:43:03,514
that means yes.
-Don't worry.

1007
00:43:03,548 --> 00:43:06,818
I know my job.
-Your credentials, Dr. Wernher.

1008
00:43:09,354 --> 00:43:12,323
-Just a moment!
-Uh, real bellboy, sir.

1009
00:43:12,357 --> 00:43:17,428
**

1010
00:43:17,462 --> 00:43:20,165
-Come in!

1011
00:43:20,198 --> 00:43:21,933
-You rang, sir?
-Yes.

1012
00:43:21,967 --> 00:43:24,970
You take those two bags,
and I'll take this briefcase.

1013
00:43:25,003 --> 00:43:27,973
-He forgot to say please.
-You want a cab, sir?

1014
00:43:28,006 --> 00:43:30,075
-No. I'm expecting a car,
an official car!

1015
00:43:30,108 --> 00:43:31,476
-How did you get here so fast?

1016
00:43:34,312 --> 00:43:36,748
-Meanwhile, in Estes Park,
Colorado.

1017
00:43:36,782 --> 00:43:38,616
Ooh.

1018
00:43:38,650 --> 00:43:44,389
**

1019
00:43:44,422 --> 00:43:45,623
-Dr. Wernher?

1020
00:43:45,657 --> 00:43:48,927
-I'll be filling in
for your rhesus monkey.

1021
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:50,495
-I see your hair plugs took.

1022
00:43:50,528 --> 00:43:52,163
-The general is waiting
for you, sir.

1023
00:43:52,197 --> 00:43:53,398
-Thank you.
-And now, Doctor,

1024
00:43:53,431 --> 00:43:54,565
we'll rush you
through security.

1025
00:43:54,599 --> 00:43:56,634
This way, please.
Sergeant, bags.

1026
00:43:56,668 --> 00:44:01,006
**

1027
00:44:01,039 --> 00:44:03,975
-SPACOM, wood fill
and meat substitute.

1028
00:44:04,009 --> 00:44:06,745
-We built this city of SPACOM!

1029
00:44:06,778 --> 00:44:09,848
-Instead of Rock and Roll?
-Yup.

1030
00:44:09,881 --> 00:44:12,984
-You see, Bill, according
to my calculus,

1031
00:44:13,018 --> 00:44:15,186
36 million miles should be equal

1032
00:44:15,220 --> 00:44:17,488
to the ratio
of 67 million miles.

1033
00:44:17,522 --> 00:44:19,725
-Cut the crap, Doctor.
I love you.

1034
00:44:19,758 --> 00:44:21,492
-Look!
It's Dr. Bellows!

1035
00:44:21,526 --> 00:44:23,161
-Yes?
What is it?

1036
00:44:23,194 --> 00:44:25,831
-Dr. Wernher has arrived and is
going through security, sir.

1037
00:44:25,864 --> 00:44:27,899
-Good! Tell him I'll meet him
for the briefing as soon

1038
00:44:27,933 --> 00:44:29,801
as possible,
say, within a half hour.

1039
00:44:29,835 --> 00:44:32,570
-"Within a half hour."
-Well, that's a break.

1040
00:44:32,603 --> 00:44:34,505
I was afraid we'd have to cancel
just because one,

1041
00:44:34,539 --> 00:44:36,641
uh, confounded civilian
was gonna be late!

1042
00:44:36,674 --> 00:44:38,576
-If you'd please, sir,

1043
00:44:38,609 --> 00:44:40,678
I don't really see why we have
to take him at all.

1044
00:44:40,712 --> 00:44:42,013
Macintosh and I can photograph

1045
00:44:42,047 --> 00:44:43,414
the back face of the Moon
without help.

1046
00:44:43,448 --> 00:44:47,118
-Ah, a lot of politics
in these things, boy.

1047
00:44:47,152 --> 00:44:48,119
If we hadn't played the science
angle,

1048
00:44:48,153 --> 00:44:49,755
we wouldn't have
gotten the authorization.

1049
00:44:49,788 --> 00:44:51,656
-Funny how science, uh,
figures into space travel.

1050
00:44:51,689 --> 00:44:53,124
-This round the moon flight
is a necessary step

1051
00:44:53,158 --> 00:44:55,493
to the establishment
of a lunar base,

1052
00:44:55,526 --> 00:44:57,428
and you know how badly
the country needs that base.

1053
00:44:57,462 --> 00:44:59,497
-I do, but could you
go over it again?

1054
00:44:59,530 --> 00:45:01,833
-And I'm mighty pleased
that you are to be the pilot.

1055
00:45:01,867 --> 00:45:04,069
Oh, I hated to bump you
out of the honor

1056
00:45:04,102 --> 00:45:07,472
of making the first orbital
flight, Bill, but, well,

1057
00:45:07,505 --> 00:45:10,175
it just had to be Briteis.
You know our reasons.

1058
00:45:10,208 --> 00:45:12,811
Well, that's years past
and long done with, sir.

1059
00:45:12,844 --> 00:45:14,612
Briteis is a good pilot.

1060
00:45:14,645 --> 00:45:16,782
I'd be the first to admit it.

1061
00:45:16,815 --> 00:45:19,951
-You used to
like Briteis, eh?

1062
00:45:19,985 --> 00:45:22,988
-Frankly, sir, Captain Briteis
was a nice kid,

1063
00:45:23,021 --> 00:45:26,257
but, well, Colonel Briteis
is a little hard to swallow.

1064
00:45:26,291 --> 00:45:28,326
-Well, Lindberg got promoted
from captain

1065
00:45:28,359 --> 00:45:29,995
to colonel for less.

1066
00:45:30,028 --> 00:45:32,663
The president simply followed
the established precedent.

1067
00:45:32,697 --> 00:45:34,732
-I suppose so, sir.

1068
00:45:34,766 --> 00:45:36,334
Anyhow, this makes up for it.

1069
00:45:36,367 --> 00:45:37,668
Being the first man to fly
around the Moon

1070
00:45:37,702 --> 00:45:40,839
is all any pilot can ask for.

1071
00:45:40,872 --> 00:45:45,276
-Uh, your doughnut wrapper, sir.
-Thank you, Private Pyle.

1072
00:45:45,310 --> 00:45:47,913
-This is from Bismarck!

1073
00:45:47,946 --> 00:45:49,781
-Oh, I hate communiques
in Abu Dhabi.

1074
00:45:49,815 --> 00:45:51,149
Let's see it.

1075
00:45:51,182 --> 00:45:54,485
Round moon flight period
Briteis reporting

1076
00:45:54,519 --> 00:45:59,390
to SPACOM
soonest period this change...

1077
00:45:59,424 --> 00:46:01,426
-Sounds like they're
still typing it.

1078
00:46:01,459 --> 00:46:03,628
-Notify me immediately
on Colonel Briteis' arrival

1079
00:46:03,661 --> 00:46:04,662
and cut the orders accordingly.

1080
00:46:04,695 --> 00:46:05,696
-Yes, sir.

1081
00:46:08,934 --> 00:46:10,368
-Your doughnut wrapper, sir?

1082
00:46:13,771 --> 00:46:15,273
-Oh, no, sir.
Not again!

1083
00:46:15,306 --> 00:46:16,341
They can't do this, sir!

1084
00:46:16,374 --> 00:46:17,742
-Sit down, Bill,
and take it easy.

1085
00:46:17,775 --> 00:46:19,677
You saw where
those others originated?

1086
00:46:19,710 --> 00:46:20,812
-Yes, sir, but I --

1087
00:46:20,846 --> 00:46:22,914
-I said sit down, Bill,
and take it easy!

1088
00:46:22,948 --> 00:46:25,783
-Sitting, sir.
Taking it easy, sir.

1089
00:46:25,817 --> 00:46:28,186
-Here, have a smoke.
-Uh, smoking --

1090
00:46:28,219 --> 00:46:30,455
Excuse me, sir.
[Intercom buzzes]

1091
00:46:30,488 --> 00:46:31,923
-Sir, there are 50 reporters
outside with about

1092
00:46:31,957 --> 00:46:33,558
six truckloads of cameras.

1093
00:46:33,591 --> 00:46:34,625
They tell me Briteis
is going to --

1094
00:46:34,659 --> 00:46:36,895
-Yes, I know, Bob.

1095
00:46:36,928 --> 00:46:38,563
Route them through PRO
and security

1096
00:46:38,596 --> 00:46:39,831
and set up the briefing room.

1097
00:46:39,865 --> 00:46:41,132
-Then turn a hose on them.

1098
00:46:41,166 --> 00:46:42,633
-...to get this through
before blastoff.

1099
00:46:45,937 --> 00:46:49,340
-Oh, Bill.
-Yes, sir?

1100
00:46:49,374 --> 00:46:52,810
-I'm sending you as copilot.
-What?

1101
00:46:52,844 --> 00:46:54,779
No, sir!

1102
00:46:54,812 --> 00:46:57,949
I mean, well, excuse me, sir,
but if it's all the same to you,

1103
00:46:57,983 --> 00:46:59,350
leave Macintosh as copilot.

1104
00:46:59,384 --> 00:47:01,686
-He's a good apple.
-Just assumed I'll have honor.

1105
00:47:01,719 --> 00:47:04,255
Besides, well, it wouldn't
be fair to Macintosh.

1106
00:47:04,289 --> 00:47:06,091
-It's not a matter
of being fair!

1107
00:47:06,124 --> 00:47:09,160
This isn't some schoolboy game!

1108
00:47:09,194 --> 00:47:11,129
This trip has got
to be successful!

1109
00:47:11,162 --> 00:47:12,130
Besides, I have
more confidence in you

1110
00:47:12,163 --> 00:47:14,832
than I have
in Macintosh or Briteis.

1111
00:47:14,866 --> 00:47:17,068
-But if it were
any other pilot, sir?

1112
00:47:17,102 --> 00:47:18,904
-Of course, I could go through
the joint chiefs

1113
00:47:18,937 --> 00:47:20,238
to the president, make a score,

1114
00:47:20,271 --> 00:47:22,941
but that would mean
postponing the flight.

1115
00:47:22,974 --> 00:47:25,977
-That would be groveling, and
you know how I hate to do that.

1116
00:47:26,011 --> 00:47:29,180
-I'll do it.
-Good boy.

1117
00:47:29,214 --> 00:47:30,748
Thanks, Bill.
-Have a biscuit, Bill.

1118
00:47:30,781 --> 00:47:32,017
-No, biscuits make me...

1119
00:47:32,050 --> 00:47:35,921
[Intercom buzzes]
Excuse me. I'm sorry.

1120
00:47:35,954 --> 00:47:39,757
-Colonel Briteis is here, sir.
-Send the colonel in.

1121
00:47:39,790 --> 00:47:41,492
-Polly Prattles is
with the colonel, sir.

1122
00:47:41,526 --> 00:47:43,628
-What?!

1123
00:47:43,661 --> 00:47:45,263
-Send Polly Prattles
to Pittsburgh!

1124
00:47:45,296 --> 00:47:46,631
Pittsburgh is perfect
for the prissy

1125
00:47:46,664 --> 00:47:48,967
pressed prima donna with pressed
aluminum party pants.

1126
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:50,936
-Beauty.
-Colonel Briteis,

1127
00:47:50,969 --> 00:47:53,138
reporting as ordered, sir.

1128
00:47:53,171 --> 00:47:56,807
-And the beast!
-My dear general.

1129
00:47:56,841 --> 00:47:57,909
-She enters the scene
like a float.

1130
00:47:57,943 --> 00:47:59,310
-Oh, how handsome you look!

1131
00:47:59,344 --> 00:48:01,012
You know, I told the president
I was so delighted...

1132
00:48:01,046 --> 00:48:02,313
-And I'm not talking root beer.

1133
00:48:02,347 --> 00:48:03,314
-...for this opportunity
of seeing you again!

1134
00:48:03,348 --> 00:48:05,183
-Does the phrase "drop anchor"
mean anything?

1135
00:48:05,216 --> 00:48:06,684
-Well, thank you, Miss Prattles.
-[Imitates foghorn]

1136
00:48:06,717 --> 00:48:08,653
-We're have a press conference
in a few minutes.

1137
00:48:08,686 --> 00:48:11,889
-Oh, but, General, I hoped
you might give me an exclusive.

1138
00:48:11,923 --> 00:48:12,657
-I'm very sorry, Miss Prattles.

1139
00:48:12,690 --> 00:48:14,325
It's policy, you know.

1140
00:48:14,359 --> 00:48:16,661
-The president told Polly
she could interview us.

1141
00:48:16,694 --> 00:48:18,763
-What?

1142
00:48:18,796 --> 00:48:21,566
Oh, very well, Miss Prattles.
I've give you 15 minutes here

1143
00:48:21,599 --> 00:48:22,934
before the meeting
in the briefing room.

1144
00:48:22,968 --> 00:48:24,802
Will that do?
-Oh, just lovely!

1145
00:48:24,835 --> 00:48:26,004
But in the meantime, let's --

1146
00:48:26,037 --> 00:48:27,138
-We're all set up
for the press conference

1147
00:48:27,172 --> 00:48:28,773
and TV broadcast, sir.

1148
00:48:28,806 --> 00:48:30,275
-Well, thanks, Charlie.

1149
00:48:30,308 --> 00:48:32,177
Oh, will you take Miss Prattles
and Major Moore for a cup

1150
00:48:32,210 --> 00:48:34,312
of coffee and bring them back
here in about 20 minutes?

1151
00:48:34,345 --> 00:48:37,248
I have a few classified matters
to discuss with the colonel --

1152
00:48:37,282 --> 00:48:39,517
highly classified.

1153
00:48:39,550 --> 00:48:41,452
-Spankin' time!

1154
00:48:45,323 --> 00:48:48,126
-Colonel, I'm sending Major
Moore as your copilot.

1155
00:48:48,159 --> 00:48:49,160
-Bill Moore?

1156
00:48:49,194 --> 00:48:51,229
Oh, no, General!
You can't.

1157
00:48:51,262 --> 00:48:53,631
-And why not?
-The big lug hates me!

1158
00:48:53,664 --> 00:48:55,266
He's jealous of me.

1159
00:48:55,300 --> 00:48:57,302
No, you'll have to
pick someone else.

1160
00:48:57,335 --> 00:48:58,736
-Now you listen to me,
Bright Eyes.

1161
00:48:58,769 --> 00:49:00,905
-Briteis, if you please.

1162
00:49:00,938 --> 00:49:03,308
-Shut up, Bright Eyes,
and listen to me!

1163
00:49:03,341 --> 00:49:05,243
Major Moore is the best pilot
we have, better than you are.

1164
00:49:05,276 --> 00:49:06,177
-But I don't understand
why you --

1165
00:49:06,211 --> 00:49:07,979
-Pipe down!
-Then be quiet.

1166
00:49:08,013 --> 00:49:10,181
-If he'd weighed 90 pounds
instead of 180...

1167
00:49:10,215 --> 00:49:11,582
-Then he's be my prom date.
-...he'd be the colonel

1168
00:49:11,616 --> 00:49:13,084
and a public hero,
and you'd still be a captain.

1169
00:49:13,118 --> 00:49:15,186
But you got the orbital flight!

1170
00:49:15,220 --> 00:49:17,422
You got the ticker-tape parades
and all the rest.

1171
00:49:17,455 --> 00:49:19,090
And ever since...
-You've gotten your own room,

1172
00:49:19,124 --> 00:49:23,628
the prince's phone,
the TV whenever you wanted it!

1173
00:49:23,661 --> 00:49:27,465
-One, colonels don't say
"no" to generals.

1174
00:49:27,498 --> 00:49:31,336
Two, you're not a superwoman.
You're a spoiled brat.

1175
00:49:31,369 --> 00:49:34,172
Three, anymore guff out of you,

1176
00:49:34,205 --> 00:49:35,506
and I'll turn you over my knee
and spank you!

1177
00:49:35,540 --> 00:49:38,109
-If you do, I'll shout
the whole place down!

1178
00:49:38,143 --> 00:49:40,345
-I might add that this room
is soundproof.

1179
00:49:40,378 --> 00:49:42,480
-You wouldn't dare!
-You want to try me?

1180
00:49:42,513 --> 00:49:45,016
-I spanked your copilot,
didn't I?

1181
00:49:45,050 --> 00:49:46,451
-No. No, sir.

1182
00:49:46,484 --> 00:49:48,019
-We better get out of here.
-All right.

1183
00:49:48,053 --> 00:49:51,156
-Spanking really is protocol
in the upper echelons of NASA.

1184
00:49:51,189 --> 00:49:55,293
-Let's talk about this flight,
shall we?

1185
00:49:55,326 --> 00:49:59,630
**

1186
00:50:01,666 --> 00:50:04,935
-As illustrated in this film --
Thank you, Cambot --

1187
00:50:04,969 --> 00:50:06,837
the tie of the future
will be cut short

1188
00:50:06,871 --> 00:50:09,340
to eliminate
the age-old morning question --

1189
00:50:09,374 --> 00:50:13,111
Is it appropriate to tuck
my tie into my pants?

1190
00:50:13,144 --> 00:50:14,745
In the future,
it won't even be an option.

1191
00:50:14,779 --> 00:50:16,681
In fact, there'll be no room
in the future

1192
00:50:16,714 --> 00:50:17,982
for fashion blunders.

1193
00:50:18,015 --> 00:50:21,752
When the short guy, a go-go tie,
swings into high,

1194
00:50:21,786 --> 00:50:23,321
but what others roads
will the tie

1195
00:50:23,354 --> 00:50:25,790
take in days of future past?

1196
00:50:25,823 --> 00:50:28,926
To start things off, Tom Servo,
who we call Cheeky,

1197
00:50:28,959 --> 00:50:31,462
is sporting
the Hexfield Body Tie

1198
00:50:31,496 --> 00:50:34,099
from the year 4000.
That's right.

1199
00:50:34,132 --> 00:50:37,235
Whether you're inside,
outside, or upside down,

1200
00:50:37,268 --> 00:50:40,138
the Hexfield Body Tie
fits and fits.

1201
00:50:40,171 --> 00:50:42,073
Very nice, Tom. Thank you.
-Don't talk to me.

1202
00:50:42,107 --> 00:50:43,808
I'm a high-fashion model
from the future.

1203
00:50:43,841 --> 00:50:45,343
-Sorry, Tom.

1204
00:50:45,376 --> 00:50:48,313
I'm wearing the Antigravi-tie.

1205
00:50:48,346 --> 00:50:50,315
This tie is a brainchild
of the defective

1206
00:50:50,348 --> 00:50:53,184
Fashion Jackal's Direct
To You store.

1207
00:50:53,218 --> 00:50:55,186
It implies
to the travel-conscious

1208
00:50:55,220 --> 00:50:59,124
that you're no stranger
to antigravity space travel.

1209
00:50:59,157 --> 00:51:03,094
Pop-up sleeves are optional.

1210
00:51:03,128 --> 00:51:06,431
Crow, from the planet
Ulio Kuryakyn...

1211
00:51:06,464 --> 00:51:08,499
-[Slurping]
-...rages on into the year 9000

1212
00:51:08,533 --> 00:51:12,637
with a tie that acknowledges
men will always dribble.

1213
00:51:12,670 --> 00:51:14,972
It senses the soup on your lips,

1214
00:51:15,005 --> 00:51:17,608
reaches up, and dabs
the soup away.

1215
00:51:17,642 --> 00:51:19,810
-[Smooching] Ahh.

1216
00:51:19,844 --> 00:51:21,679
-And now, our own Tom Servo
is back,

1217
00:51:21,712 --> 00:51:23,080
just out of the dressing room,

1218
00:51:23,114 --> 00:51:26,484
I might add, in the nick of time
with the ultimate tie

1219
00:51:26,517 --> 00:51:29,454
designed for our friends from
The Church of the Future

1220
00:51:29,487 --> 00:51:31,856
Machismo Irony.

1221
00:51:31,889 --> 00:51:35,493
They guarantee this will stay
stylish for millennia to come.

1222
00:51:35,526 --> 00:51:37,595
This tie is from the
Warp-Burning Plastic

1223
00:51:37,628 --> 00:51:41,132
Collection, again, from those
fashion drones on Maja Five.

1224
00:51:41,166 --> 00:51:46,404
This tie automatically lengthens
to ever-changing ties

1225
00:51:46,437 --> 00:51:48,673
and trends of fashion.
-[Klaxons blaring]

1226
00:51:48,706 --> 00:51:52,643
Oh! We got movie sign!
Let's cheese and let's go!

1227
00:51:52,677 --> 00:51:56,481
[Doors whir]

1228
00:51:56,514 --> 00:52:00,685
[Doors screech and creak]

1229
00:52:00,718 --> 00:52:03,288
-A rocket flight from the Earth
directly to the Moon...

1230
00:52:03,321 --> 00:52:04,655
-I thought that tie thing
was funny.

1231
00:52:04,689 --> 00:52:08,993
I don't know about you.
-I thought it was great.

1232
00:52:09,026 --> 00:52:13,231
-...revolving around the Earth,
a satellite view, I might add,

1233
00:52:13,264 --> 00:52:15,633
directly to the heroic
first orbital flight

1234
00:52:15,666 --> 00:52:20,505
by Colonel Briteis 4 years ago.

1235
00:52:20,538 --> 00:52:22,340
Now that we have
a space station...

1236
00:52:22,373 --> 00:52:23,674
-Or a Frisbee.

1237
00:52:23,708 --> 00:52:26,577
-...it is, at last,
possible to send a ship...

1238
00:52:26,611 --> 00:52:28,145
-Or batteries.

1239
00:52:28,179 --> 00:52:29,647
-...all the way
around the Moon.

1240
00:52:29,680 --> 00:52:33,251
-Or playground ball.
-On this trip,

1241
00:52:33,284 --> 00:52:35,520
Dr. Wernher will photograph
the back face...

1242
00:52:35,553 --> 00:52:37,755
-Or double.

1243
00:52:37,788 --> 00:52:40,057
-The ship will then return
to the space station.

1244
00:52:40,090 --> 00:52:43,127
-Or Frisbee.
-I'm so glad...

1245
00:52:43,160 --> 00:52:45,162
-Or a large barge.
-...and so will

1246
00:52:45,196 --> 00:52:46,497
all my readers be,

1247
00:52:46,531 --> 00:52:49,400
but, General,
what's the purpose of all this?

1248
00:52:49,434 --> 00:52:51,101
-Uh, what do you mean,
Miss Prattles?

1249
00:52:51,135 --> 00:52:54,305
-Well, my editor...
Oh, an old bear.

1250
00:52:54,339 --> 00:52:55,906
Nothing ever suits him.

1251
00:52:55,940 --> 00:52:58,343
Well, he says this whole thing
is a bulldoggle,

1252
00:52:58,376 --> 00:53:00,845
just another way
of wasting tax money.

1253
00:53:00,878 --> 00:53:02,947
Now what am I to tell him?
-Tosh!

1254
00:53:02,980 --> 00:53:04,181
Peccable!
-A fair question, Miss Prattles.

1255
00:53:04,215 --> 00:53:05,516
-Mummery! Poppycock!

1256
00:53:05,550 --> 00:53:07,585
-This round the moon flight
is a necessary step

1257
00:53:07,618 --> 00:53:09,487
before establishing
a base on the moon.

1258
00:53:09,520 --> 00:53:12,122
It's a survey flight.

1259
00:53:12,156 --> 00:53:14,492
Maybe someday the statesmen...
-You're so military.

1260
00:53:14,525 --> 00:53:17,495
I bet he gives good spankings.

1261
00:53:17,528 --> 00:53:20,097
-But, in the meantime,
if there is going to be a base

1262
00:53:20,130 --> 00:53:23,434
on the moon,
and there will be...

1263
00:53:23,468 --> 00:53:26,771
-I want it to be first base.
-...see that it's in safe hands.

1264
00:53:26,804 --> 00:53:29,139
-I'm not even
supposed to be here.

1265
00:53:29,173 --> 00:53:32,310
-Ma'am, the most important thing
in the world to me

1266
00:53:32,343 --> 00:53:34,512
is the military security
of the United States.

1267
00:53:34,545 --> 00:53:36,080
-And my dog, Floppy.

1268
00:53:36,113 --> 00:53:39,083
-And I'm not in the least
bit apologetic for my attitude.

1269
00:53:39,116 --> 00:53:40,618
-I wonder, General,
could you tell me

1270
00:53:40,651 --> 00:53:42,253
something about this wonderful
space station?

1271
00:53:42,287 --> 00:53:43,821
-I'll help you
with the cue cards.

1272
00:53:43,854 --> 00:53:45,990
-Yes. That would be
very interesting, General.

1273
00:53:46,023 --> 00:53:47,958
-Well, I can give you
a rough idea.

1274
00:53:47,992 --> 00:53:50,295
The station is a titanium hull

1275
00:53:50,328 --> 00:53:56,166
with steel bracing,
350 feet in diameter.

1276
00:53:56,200 --> 00:53:57,602
It rotates completely around
the Earth

1277
00:53:57,635 --> 00:54:01,138
in the transpolar orbit
about 10 times a day.

1278
00:54:03,508 --> 00:54:05,610
At present, the station
is in a state of free fall.

1279
00:54:05,643 --> 00:54:07,011
-Free fall?

1280
00:54:07,044 --> 00:54:08,313
Could you explain
that a little more?

1281
00:54:08,346 --> 00:54:09,680
-Well, of course.
When we speak of free fall,

1282
00:54:09,714 --> 00:54:12,517
we simply mean that the station
and everything in it

1283
00:54:12,550 --> 00:54:16,587
are moving around the Earth
at a speed which is great enough

1284
00:54:16,621 --> 00:54:19,924
so that it cannot
fall toward the Earth,

1285
00:54:19,957 --> 00:54:25,430
but it is also moving at a speed
which is not great enough

1286
00:54:25,463 --> 00:54:28,666
in order to enable it
to go out into space.

1287
00:54:28,699 --> 00:54:35,005
Therefore, the forces of gravity
and speed are now in balance.

1288
00:54:35,039 --> 00:54:38,142
-That was very good.
Yay!

1289
00:54:38,175 --> 00:54:41,245
You made it through.
-No weight?

1290
00:54:41,278 --> 00:54:43,348
What a wonderful idea!

1291
00:54:43,381 --> 00:54:44,649
-Got to be hard
for her to imagine.

1292
00:54:44,682 --> 00:54:45,816
-I wouldn't weigh anything?

1293
00:54:45,850 --> 00:54:47,652
-Nothing at all.

1294
00:54:47,685 --> 00:54:49,620
As a matter of fact, you'd have
to wear magnetic shoes

1295
00:54:49,654 --> 00:54:50,988
to keep you on the floor.

1296
00:54:51,021 --> 00:54:53,458
-Why, I have a pink pair
of those at home!

1297
00:54:53,491 --> 00:54:54,559
-...or the ceiling.

1298
00:54:54,592 --> 00:54:56,327
-I wonder, General,
could I arrange

1299
00:54:56,361 --> 00:54:59,397
to go there,
to the space station?

1300
00:54:59,430 --> 00:55:01,732
-Well, hardly, Miss Prattles.

1301
00:55:01,766 --> 00:55:04,802
-There is the little problem
of escape velocity, ma'am.

1302
00:55:04,835 --> 00:55:08,573
-...just about $300 a pound
to send anything to the station,

1303
00:55:08,606 --> 00:55:10,775
and, consequently,

1304
00:55:10,808 --> 00:55:13,444
our personnel must all weigh
less than 160 pounds.

1305
00:55:13,478 --> 00:55:15,346
-You're over by a metric ton!

1306
00:55:15,380 --> 00:55:17,682
-It would be so lovely
to weigh nothing at all.

1307
00:55:17,715 --> 00:55:19,149
[Both laughing]

1308
00:55:19,183 --> 00:55:21,619
-I've heard of the H-bomb,
General.

1309
00:55:21,652 --> 00:55:24,355
Isn't it dangerous living
and working so near to it?

1310
00:55:24,389 --> 00:55:26,056
-I'm very sorry, Doctor,

1311
00:55:26,090 --> 00:55:28,292
but that's
a highly classified subject.

1312
00:55:28,325 --> 00:55:30,895
-A simple yes or no
would do, dickweed.

1313
00:55:30,928 --> 00:55:33,598
**

1314
00:55:33,631 --> 00:55:36,166
-[Imitates drumming]

1315
00:55:36,200 --> 00:55:38,068
-Isn't she something?
-Yeah!

1316
00:55:38,102 --> 00:55:40,237
And the girl ain't bad, either.

1317
00:55:40,270 --> 00:55:43,574
-General Greene, your rocket
ship, number three, Canada,

1318
00:55:43,608 --> 00:55:45,576
ready for takeoff
to space station.

1319
00:55:45,610 --> 00:55:49,079
-No diving or splashing, please,
and no running on the deck!

1320
00:55:49,113 --> 00:55:50,180
-Happy landing, sir.

1321
00:55:50,214 --> 00:55:52,750
-Colonel Briteis,
your ship is fueled.

1322
00:55:52,783 --> 00:55:54,318
Check-off list completed.

1323
00:55:54,351 --> 00:55:57,187
-Do you need any help?
-No, thank you, Major.

1324
00:55:57,221 --> 00:56:00,290
I'm perfectly capable of getting
into the rocket myself.

1325
00:56:00,324 --> 00:56:03,461
-Well, all right,
Miss Gloria Steinem.

1326
00:56:05,796 --> 00:56:07,064
-That's Ms. Gloria Steinem.

1327
00:56:07,097 --> 00:56:08,933
-Oh, right. Yeah.

1328
00:56:08,966 --> 00:56:11,569
-Well, there's only one bunk.
[Laughs]

1329
00:56:11,602 --> 00:56:15,072
-May I strap you in, Colonel?
-No, thank you, Major!

1330
00:56:15,105 --> 00:56:16,741
Are your ship-to-ship
and ship-to-blockhouse

1331
00:56:16,774 --> 00:56:18,576
frequencies operating?

1332
00:56:18,609 --> 00:56:20,344
-Shipshape!
-...Colonel.

1333
00:56:20,377 --> 00:56:22,179
Orders are to keep
the channels cleared

1334
00:56:22,212 --> 00:56:24,081
until the Canada blastoff.

1335
00:56:24,114 --> 00:56:25,750
-Canada to Blockhouse.

1336
00:56:25,783 --> 00:56:28,753
Request permission
to stop pumps.

1337
00:56:28,786 --> 00:56:31,188
-Sorry, uh, but Canada is dry.

1338
00:56:31,221 --> 00:56:33,223
-Request permission
to blast off.

1339
00:56:33,257 --> 00:56:34,725
-Permission granted.
-Hey, Canada.

1340
00:56:34,759 --> 00:56:37,327
Take off, you hosers.

1341
00:56:37,361 --> 00:56:42,967
-Our subject today is space
launches, so, uh,

1342
00:56:43,000 --> 00:56:45,636
we're gonna have a takeoff.
Hey, you get it?

1343
00:56:45,670 --> 00:56:48,005
You know, it's kind of like
a rocket is going to take off,

1344
00:56:48,038 --> 00:56:50,140
and then they say
take off on that bit.

1345
00:56:52,477 --> 00:56:54,945
-Look at that takeoff.
-Oh, good day to you, then.

1346
00:56:54,979 --> 00:57:01,986
**

1347
00:57:02,019 --> 00:57:09,126
**

1348
00:57:09,159 --> 00:57:12,262
-You may start blastoff
procedure, Captain.

1349
00:57:12,296 --> 00:57:15,232
-Thank you, Colonel.
-Yes, Colonel.

1350
00:57:15,265 --> 00:57:17,001
-Mexico to Blockhouse.

1351
00:57:17,034 --> 00:57:19,003
Request permission
to start pumps.

1352
00:57:19,036 --> 00:57:20,104
[Beeping]

1353
00:57:20,137 --> 00:57:23,474
-Permission granted.

1354
00:57:23,508 --> 00:57:25,843
-Mexico to Blockhouse.
-Make a run for the border!

1355
00:57:25,876 --> 00:57:27,845
-...permission to blast off.

1356
00:57:27,878 --> 00:57:30,815
-Blockhouse to Mexico.
Clearance granted.

1357
00:57:30,848 --> 00:57:32,917
You are minus 10 seconds.

1358
00:57:32,950 --> 00:57:35,520
9, 8...
-3.

1359
00:57:35,553 --> 00:57:36,687
-...7...
-13.

1360
00:57:36,721 --> 00:57:37,588
-...6...
-9.

1361
00:57:37,622 --> 00:57:39,256
-...5...
-22.

1362
00:57:39,289 --> 00:57:40,457
-...4...
-33.

1363
00:57:40,491 --> 00:57:41,291
-...3...
-1,000,005.

1364
00:57:41,325 --> 00:57:43,293
-Hut!
-...2, 1.

1365
00:57:43,327 --> 00:57:45,496
-Don't blow it!
It's your big chance.

1366
00:57:45,530 --> 00:57:51,168
**

1367
00:57:51,201 --> 00:57:52,803
Whoa!

1368
00:57:52,837 --> 00:57:54,739
Ooh, Buddy Ebsen's happy.

1369
00:57:54,772 --> 00:57:58,809
-Aah!

1370
00:57:58,843 --> 00:58:00,978
-She's absolutely ballistic!

1371
00:58:01,011 --> 00:58:08,018
**

1372
00:58:08,052 --> 00:58:09,820
[Beeping]

1373
00:58:09,854 --> 00:58:13,591
-Base control to Mexico.
Prepare to receive line.

1374
00:58:13,624 --> 00:58:15,826
-Looks like Deep 13, kind of.

1375
00:58:15,860 --> 00:58:21,065
-[Vocalizing]

1376
00:58:21,098 --> 00:58:23,433
-...to Lock 3.
Bring them in.

1377
00:58:23,467 --> 00:58:27,471
-[Vocalizing]

1378
00:58:27,504 --> 00:58:31,609
**

1379
00:58:31,642 --> 00:58:33,811
-Welcome to Whambo World.

1380
00:58:33,844 --> 00:58:42,519
**

1381
00:58:42,553 --> 00:58:44,789
-That's right.

1382
00:58:44,822 --> 00:58:48,492
**

1383
00:58:48,525 --> 00:58:50,460
That ought to do it.

1384
00:58:50,494 --> 00:58:53,831
-Lock 3 to Mexico.
You are secured.

1385
00:58:53,864 --> 00:58:55,600
You may disembark.

1386
00:58:55,633 --> 00:58:58,268
Over.

1387
00:58:58,302 --> 00:59:00,838
-Mexico to Lock 3.
Disembarking.

1388
00:59:00,871 --> 00:59:03,073
-[Barking]

1389
00:59:03,107 --> 00:59:05,542
-[Snarling]
-[Whimpers]

1390
00:59:09,213 --> 00:59:12,583
-I must report immediately.
See you before you leave.

1391
00:59:12,617 --> 00:59:14,451
-I'll be with you as far
as weight control.

1392
00:59:14,484 --> 00:59:15,686
-I don't need any
weight control!

1393
00:59:15,720 --> 00:59:17,955
Maybe a tummy tuck or something.

1394
00:59:17,988 --> 00:59:19,489
This is all muscle!

1395
00:59:19,523 --> 00:59:27,331
**

1396
00:59:27,364 --> 00:59:29,734
-Seaman winds up.
-Listen.

1397
00:59:29,767 --> 00:59:34,004
-Here's the pitch, and it's
a home run for Brooklyn!

1398
00:59:34,038 --> 00:59:36,240
-Back to business, Major.
-Aw.

1399
00:59:36,273 --> 00:59:37,808
This is the future
when they sold the Dodgers

1400
00:59:37,842 --> 00:59:40,010
back to Brooklyn.

1401
00:59:40,044 --> 00:59:42,146
-Hope you enjoy the trip,
Colonel.

1402
00:59:42,179 --> 00:59:44,414
Happy landings, Bill.
-Thank you, Captain.

1403
00:59:44,448 --> 00:59:46,817
-Oh, you dropped your pen.
Shoe is untied.

1404
00:59:46,851 --> 00:59:49,419
[Laughs]
I love this.

1405
00:59:49,453 --> 00:59:51,488
-Hey, don't cross
the split screen.

1406
00:59:51,521 --> 00:59:53,624
-Art design by M.C. Escher.

1407
00:59:56,961 --> 00:59:59,630
-Whoa.
-Whoa.

1408
00:59:59,664 --> 01:00:01,799
-Please be seated.
-Thank you, sir.

1409
01:00:01,832 --> 01:00:02,933
-Nice to see you again,

1410
01:00:02,967 --> 01:00:05,135
Colonel, Major.
-Major.

1411
01:00:05,169 --> 01:00:06,503
-Colonel.
-Doctor.

1412
01:00:06,536 --> 01:00:07,638
-Please strap yourself
in, Colonel.

1413
01:00:07,672 --> 01:00:08,673
-Captain?
-...about these things.

1414
01:00:08,706 --> 01:00:10,174
You're in free fall.

1415
01:00:13,043 --> 01:00:15,079
The General has asked me
to brief you

1416
01:00:15,112 --> 01:00:17,114
on the conditions of this shoot.

1417
01:00:17,147 --> 01:00:20,450
As you know, it's to be
a circumlunar flight.

1418
01:00:20,484 --> 01:00:24,021
Colonel Briteis will pilot.
Major Moore, copilot.

1419
01:00:24,054 --> 01:00:26,724
Dr. Wernher will do the
photographic and scientific work

1420
01:00:26,757 --> 01:00:29,126
as you pass around
the back face of the Moon.

1421
01:00:29,159 --> 01:00:31,095
-I think we should attempt
a landing, sir.

1422
01:00:31,128 --> 01:00:33,630
-Shut up.
-One step at a time, Colonel.

1423
01:00:33,664 --> 01:00:35,733
There will be only
a 25-second margin of fuel over

1424
01:00:35,766 --> 01:00:37,968
and above calculated
as necessary for the flight,

1425
01:00:38,002 --> 01:00:40,470
a resumption of the orbit
with the station.

1426
01:00:40,504 --> 01:00:42,072
Therefore, you must proceed
exactly

1427
01:00:42,106 --> 01:00:44,074
according to your flight plan.

1428
01:00:44,108 --> 01:00:46,176
If you make this one all right,

1429
01:00:46,210 --> 01:00:48,112
we may give you a crack
at the first Moon landing.

1430
01:00:48,145 --> 01:00:50,180
[Intercom chimes]
-Briteis for 50 points.

1431
01:00:50,214 --> 01:00:51,749
-This is Lock 4, sir.
The Magellan is fueled

1432
01:00:51,782 --> 01:00:53,150
and ready according to plan.

1433
01:00:53,183 --> 01:00:55,152
Check-off list completed.
[Intercom chimes]

1434
01:00:55,185 --> 01:00:59,023
-Bill for 20 points.
-You blast off in 37 minutes.

1435
01:00:59,056 --> 01:01:00,657
It's yours, Colonel.

1436
01:01:00,691 --> 01:01:01,792
Man your ship.
Good luck.

1437
01:01:01,826 --> 01:01:03,493
-Or woman your ship,
as it were.

1438
01:01:03,527 --> 01:01:04,461
[Chuckles]

1439
01:01:07,131 --> 01:01:10,000
-Boy, walking on the walls
must be a big problem.

1440
01:01:16,506 --> 01:01:17,775
-Speed bump.

1441
01:01:17,808 --> 01:01:23,647
**

1442
01:01:23,680 --> 01:01:25,916
-Magellan to Lock 4,
all secured.

1443
01:01:25,950 --> 01:01:27,584
Shove off.
-Same to you, toots.

1444
01:01:27,617 --> 01:01:32,256
-Lock 4 to Magellan,
shoving off on count of 10.

1445
01:01:32,289 --> 01:01:34,792
-The technical term
of shoving off.

1446
01:01:34,825 --> 01:01:44,001
**

1447
01:01:44,034 --> 01:01:46,937
-Magellan to space control,
check starting coordinates.

1448
01:01:46,971 --> 01:01:49,639
Over.
-Your coordinates are Polaris

1449
01:01:49,673 --> 01:01:54,344
83 degrees, 62 minutes,
axis A,

1450
01:01:54,378 --> 01:02:01,585
Regulus, 180 degrees,
29 minutes, axis B, Vomohol...

1451
01:02:01,618 --> 01:02:05,322
-Glutimas maximus, radius,
meaningless dribble.

1452
01:02:05,355 --> 01:02:06,656
Over.

1453
01:02:06,690 --> 01:02:09,226
-Initiate cheesy
effects sequence.

1454
01:02:09,259 --> 01:02:13,730
-Roger that.
Initiating cheesy effects, Bill.

1455
01:02:13,764 --> 01:02:17,134
-Look, they're using
View-Master technology.

1456
01:02:17,167 --> 01:02:19,569
-Battery pack separating
from Frisbee.

1457
01:02:19,603 --> 01:02:29,579
**

1458
01:02:29,613 --> 01:02:30,815
-Look, the Grand Tetons.

1459
01:02:30,848 --> 01:02:34,151
-Swing slowly right
about 2 degrees,

1460
01:02:34,184 --> 01:02:38,522
nose south, 1 degree,
a little more down.

1461
01:02:38,555 --> 01:02:39,857
Hold it.

1462
01:02:39,890 --> 01:02:41,425
And...
-Perfect.

1463
01:02:41,458 --> 01:02:44,428
[Beeping]

1464
01:02:44,461 --> 01:02:53,804
**

1465
01:02:53,838 --> 01:02:56,506
-Steady.

1466
01:02:56,540 --> 01:02:58,642
Hold it.

1467
01:02:58,675 --> 01:03:02,312
-They're having the Indian
ceremony at the Grand Canyon.

1468
01:03:02,346 --> 01:03:03,948
-Rocketship Magellan
to space control.

1469
01:03:03,981 --> 01:03:05,449
We are now correctly aligned.

1470
01:03:05,482 --> 01:03:07,017
Over.

1471
01:03:09,286 --> 01:03:12,456
-You have 13 seconds
to blastoff.

1472
01:03:12,489 --> 01:03:14,124
Mark.

1473
01:03:14,158 --> 01:03:19,763
13, 12, 11, 10,

1474
01:03:19,796 --> 01:03:23,300
9, 8, 7...

1475
01:03:23,333 --> 01:03:24,468
-5.
-...6...

1476
01:03:24,501 --> 01:03:25,635
-9
-13.

1477
01:03:25,669 --> 01:03:26,603
-8
-...5, 4...

1478
01:03:26,636 --> 01:03:28,105
-7.
-...3...

1479
01:03:28,138 --> 01:03:29,139
-6.
-...2...

1480
01:03:29,173 --> 01:03:30,941
-5.
-...1.

1481
01:03:30,975 --> 01:03:32,409
-10.
-Fire.

1482
01:03:32,442 --> 01:03:34,678
-Wow.
Free game.

1483
01:03:34,711 --> 01:03:37,147
-Duracell away,
the copper-top shuttle.

1484
01:03:37,181 --> 01:03:38,448
-Oy!

1485
01:03:38,482 --> 01:03:45,522
**

1486
01:03:45,555 --> 01:03:47,892
-Bye.

1487
01:03:47,925 --> 01:03:53,297
Bon Voyage-y.

1488
01:03:53,330 --> 01:03:54,764
-Well, numbers are
really meaningless

1489
01:03:54,798 --> 01:03:56,333
in a gravity-o-meter.

1490
01:03:56,366 --> 01:03:58,502
-Initiate meaningless lights.

1491
01:03:58,535 --> 01:04:01,105
**

1492
01:04:01,138 --> 01:04:03,240
-And up it goes.

1493
01:04:03,273 --> 01:04:13,017
**

1494
01:04:16,153 --> 01:04:19,623
-But what is controlling
the ship now?

1495
01:04:19,656 --> 01:04:21,625
-As you can see by the lights
in the tape control,

1496
01:04:21,658 --> 01:04:24,294
the autopilot is keeping us
on our computer track

1497
01:04:24,328 --> 01:04:25,795
with the ship
in its proper attitude.

1498
01:04:25,829 --> 01:04:27,831
-I see.
-Ordinarily, the rocket jets

1499
01:04:27,864 --> 01:04:29,799
are controlled by the autopilot,

1500
01:04:29,833 --> 01:04:32,502
but the pilot
can always fire by hand.

1501
01:04:32,536 --> 01:04:34,804
Now, if I were to throw
this switch,

1502
01:04:34,838 --> 01:04:39,409
I would override the automatic
pilot, and the jets would fire.

1503
01:04:39,443 --> 01:04:41,311
-So?
But how would you guide it?

1504
01:04:41,345 --> 01:04:42,746
-Well, it's broken.
-Huh?

1505
01:04:42,779 --> 01:04:44,581
-Well, you can't exactly steer
a rocket

1506
01:04:44,614 --> 01:04:46,951
since there's nothing out there
to grab hold of.

1507
01:04:46,984 --> 01:04:49,619
You must first turn it
by the fly wheel,

1508
01:04:49,653 --> 01:04:51,688
and then it is held steady
by the gyroscopes

1509
01:04:51,721 --> 01:04:53,991
while you fire the jets.

1510
01:04:54,024 --> 01:04:55,592
-And the whole thing's
held up by string.

1511
01:04:55,625 --> 01:04:57,761
-Very interesting,
very interesting indeed.

1512
01:04:57,794 --> 01:05:00,965
Thank you very much.

1513
01:05:00,998 --> 01:05:03,000
-She always gets to look
in the View-Master.

1514
01:05:05,302 --> 01:05:09,139
These people are dressed
like camp counselors.

1515
01:05:09,173 --> 01:05:10,140
-Like this?
-That can't work now.

1516
01:05:10,174 --> 01:05:12,109
The copilot board is dead.

1517
01:05:12,142 --> 01:05:16,080
-Huh?
-I wouldn't touch it.

1518
01:05:16,113 --> 01:05:20,484
-I'm sorry.
-Think nothing of it, Doctor.

1519
01:05:20,517 --> 01:05:22,452
The Major doesn't like
for someone else

1520
01:05:22,486 --> 01:05:25,189
to play with his toys,
but that's enough now.

1521
01:05:25,222 --> 01:05:27,757
I'll give you
another lesson tomorrow.

1522
01:05:27,791 --> 01:05:29,626
-Oh, get that lady
a saucer of milk.

1523
01:05:29,659 --> 01:05:31,061
-Meow.

1524
01:05:31,095 --> 01:05:40,937
**

1525
01:05:40,971 --> 01:05:43,440
-I think we have the cameras
adjusted now.

1526
01:05:43,473 --> 01:05:46,176
They can run by themselves
for an hour or two.

1527
01:05:46,210 --> 01:05:48,578
-Doctor, you haven't set
the automatic trip yet.

1528
01:05:48,612 --> 01:05:50,514
-The automatic trip, yeah.
-How stupid of me.

1529
01:05:50,547 --> 01:05:53,350
-It's hard to take anyone
in a flannel skullcap seriously.

1530
01:05:53,383 --> 01:05:56,153
-I'd be glad to fix it.
-Oh, fine.

1531
01:05:56,186 --> 01:05:58,522
And while you're back at it,
I think I'll warm up the radio.

1532
01:05:58,555 --> 01:05:59,923
We'll be back
inside of Earth soon.

1533
01:05:59,956 --> 01:06:02,459
Maybe I can find out
how the World Series came out.

1534
01:06:02,492 --> 01:06:04,061
I suppose you're a Dodger
fan, huh?

1535
01:06:04,094 --> 01:06:05,495
-Do they play for the Fuhrer?

1536
01:06:05,529 --> 01:06:06,930
Oops.

1537
01:06:06,963 --> 01:06:10,934
-Oh, I, uh, I thought
you used to teach in Brooklyn.

1538
01:06:10,967 --> 01:06:12,069
-Well, that's right.
-Yeah.

1539
01:06:12,102 --> 01:06:14,338
I mean, uh, yes.
-I'll go below.

1540
01:06:14,371 --> 01:06:17,241
Um, you see
if you can raise Earth.

1541
01:06:17,274 --> 01:06:18,575
-If I could raise the Earth,

1542
01:06:18,608 --> 01:06:22,079
I wouldn't need to go
into space, you dolt.

1543
01:06:22,112 --> 01:06:25,115
-Hey, there's no safety rail
on that access hole.

1544
01:06:25,149 --> 01:06:26,683
-He's not real.

1545
01:06:26,716 --> 01:06:28,552
He just crawled
into the septic tank.

1546
01:06:32,656 --> 01:06:36,260
-Colonel.
Colonel, wake up!

1547
01:06:36,293 --> 01:06:38,095
-What is it, Bill?

1548
01:06:38,128 --> 01:06:39,396
-That guy, Wernher,
he's a phony.

1549
01:06:39,429 --> 01:06:40,630
-What?

1550
01:06:40,664 --> 01:06:42,499
Explain yourself.
-Mm.

1551
01:06:42,532 --> 01:06:43,800
-He knows too much
about rockets

1552
01:06:43,833 --> 01:06:45,302
and not enough
about photography.

1553
01:06:45,335 --> 01:06:47,471
I know more about his cameras
than he does.

1554
01:06:47,504 --> 01:06:49,473
-I think you're
imagining things.

1555
01:06:49,506 --> 01:06:52,109
-No. Wernher's a wiener.
-I am, am I?

1556
01:06:52,142 --> 01:06:53,410
He's supposed to be
from Brooklyn,

1557
01:06:53,443 --> 01:06:55,445
and he's never even heard
of the Dodgers.

1558
01:06:55,479 --> 01:06:56,680
Look, I don't know
what his game is.

1559
01:06:56,713 --> 01:06:58,515
Maybe it's sabotage.
-So...

1560
01:06:58,548 --> 01:07:00,250
-What's there to sabotage?
-The spy comes out of the hole,

1561
01:07:00,284 --> 01:07:01,751
runs around the control panel
and goes back down in.

1562
01:07:01,785 --> 01:07:04,488
-Major, I think
you're space-happy.

1563
01:07:04,521 --> 01:07:06,323
[Gasps and screams]

1564
01:07:06,356 --> 01:07:09,059
-Oh, my God!

1565
01:07:09,093 --> 01:07:12,962
-It's Hands Down,
the slap-happiest game ever.

1566
01:07:15,599 --> 01:07:17,334
-Oh.

1567
01:07:17,367 --> 01:07:18,402
-Yeah.

1568
01:07:18,435 --> 01:07:22,172
**

1569
01:07:22,206 --> 01:07:26,110
-I am not an animal.

1570
01:07:26,143 --> 01:07:28,478
-Got your nose.

1571
01:07:28,512 --> 01:07:35,852
**

1572
01:07:35,885 --> 01:07:38,322
-While Briteis relaxes
in the chaise lounge,

1573
01:07:38,355 --> 01:07:40,457
Jim will attempt to subdue
the angry spy.

1574
01:07:40,490 --> 01:07:48,932
**

1575
01:07:48,965 --> 01:07:52,336
-This is low-impact aerobics.

1576
01:07:52,369 --> 01:07:54,471
-It's low-impact
filmmaking too.

1577
01:07:54,504 --> 01:08:01,645
**

1578
01:08:01,678 --> 01:08:04,114
Higher and higher they climb
to heights

1579
01:08:04,148 --> 01:08:08,652
they've never been to before,
and it was good.

1580
01:08:08,685 --> 01:08:15,024
-Got to push button,
got to slow down our orbit now.

1581
01:08:17,794 --> 01:08:19,396
-Up, up, up.

1582
01:08:19,429 --> 01:08:26,069
-Got to find way to get button.

1583
01:08:26,102 --> 01:08:29,139
-Thanks goodness.
-Ugh!

1584
01:08:29,173 --> 01:08:30,507
-Nice tag.

1585
01:08:30,540 --> 01:08:40,350
**

1586
01:08:40,384 --> 01:08:50,160
**

1587
01:08:50,194 --> 01:08:51,595
-Honey, come here.

1588
01:08:51,628 --> 01:08:53,263
Look, the spy looks so cute
when he's sleeping.

1589
01:08:53,297 --> 01:08:55,765
-We've got to get out of here,
come on.

1590
01:08:55,799 --> 01:08:57,567
Looks like one of
your prom dates, Joel.

1591
01:08:57,601 --> 01:08:58,668
-Thanks a lot.

1592
01:09:01,171 --> 01:09:02,772
[Doors thud and creak]

1593
01:09:02,806 --> 01:09:04,641
[Doors whir]

1594
01:09:04,674 --> 01:09:08,077
[Doors thud and clank]

1595
01:09:08,111 --> 01:09:09,479
[Door slams]

1596
01:09:09,513 --> 01:09:11,014
-It's new.
-It's improved.

1597
01:09:11,047 --> 01:09:12,682
-It's SPACOM, as seen
in the movie

1598
01:09:12,716 --> 01:09:14,651
"Project Moon Base."
-Yes, SPACOM.

1599
01:09:14,684 --> 01:09:16,286
The miracle home product
you thought you'd never need.

1600
01:09:16,320 --> 01:09:18,121
-Part wood,
part industrial resin,

1601
01:09:18,154 --> 01:09:20,324
part pasteurized processed
cheese food product.

1602
01:09:20,357 --> 01:09:22,292
-That valuable China crack?
No problem.

1603
01:09:22,326 --> 01:09:23,893
Just a dab of SPACOM
will do the trick.

1604
01:09:23,927 --> 01:09:26,463
-Try SPACOM on an onion roll
for a real lunchtime treat.

1605
01:09:26,496 --> 01:09:28,164
-Takes care of rust
on that old jalopy,

1606
01:09:28,198 --> 01:09:30,133
and ladies will just love
how SPACOM removes

1607
01:09:30,166 --> 01:09:31,801
those nagging liver spots
and plantar's warts.

1608
01:09:31,835 --> 01:09:34,271
-Slice thin for a fabulous
roast substitute.

1609
01:09:34,304 --> 01:09:35,805
-Kids will just have a ball
with SPACOM.

1610
01:09:35,839 --> 01:09:37,407
-Change your mother
into a basketball

1611
01:09:37,441 --> 01:09:39,343
and drive our rodents
and other household pets.

1612
01:09:39,376 --> 01:09:40,910
-Include SPACOM in your
next oil change

1613
01:09:40,944 --> 01:09:42,646
to lubricate
and remove diaper rash

1614
01:09:42,679 --> 01:09:44,681
while it whitens your wash
and melts those pounds away.

1615
01:09:44,714 --> 01:09:47,484
-Make Polynesian cheese devils
with a little marshmallow cream,

1616
01:09:47,517 --> 01:09:50,454
a handful of crunchy fire ants
and lots of velvety SPACOM.

1617
01:09:50,487 --> 01:09:51,821
-Eskimos love the way SPACOM

1618
01:09:51,855 --> 01:09:53,290
takes the gum
out of diesel generators.

1619
01:09:53,323 --> 01:09:55,425
-Mom loves the way
it cleans jewelry.

1620
01:09:55,459 --> 01:09:56,960
-Dad loved the way
it takes the paint

1621
01:09:56,993 --> 01:09:58,262
off that old table
in the the attic.

1622
01:09:58,295 --> 01:10:00,330
-And kids just love it
for a snack any time.

1623
01:10:00,364 --> 01:10:02,732
-Massage it into your scalp
for a refreshing change of mind.

1624
01:10:02,766 --> 01:10:03,933
-Rub it into your chest

1625
01:10:03,967 --> 01:10:05,502
and feel the petroleum vapors
go to work.

1626
01:10:05,535 --> 01:10:07,070
-Not an aerosol, not a paste

1627
01:10:07,103 --> 01:10:09,439
and not available in any stores.
-It's SPACOM!

1628
01:10:09,473 --> 01:10:10,940
And it's available at this
one-time low,

1629
01:10:10,974 --> 01:10:12,476
low everyday bargain price.

1630
01:10:12,509 --> 01:10:14,010
-And if you order now,
you'll receive at no

1631
01:10:14,043 --> 01:10:16,413
extra charge
a year's supply of SPACOM.

1632
01:10:16,446 --> 01:10:18,415
-Cuts through this tomato
like it was a tin can.

1633
01:10:18,448 --> 01:10:21,017
-Snap those caps off jars,
bottles, and the baby.

1634
01:10:21,050 --> 01:10:22,819
-And, boy, does it catch fish.

1635
01:10:22,852 --> 01:10:27,223
**

1636
01:10:30,026 --> 01:10:36,132
**

1637
01:10:36,165 --> 01:10:37,601
-You can tell they're about
to land

1638
01:10:37,634 --> 01:10:40,337
'cause the music couldn't
get any lower, you know?

1639
01:10:40,370 --> 01:10:43,707
-The lunar explorer model sits
down on the Moon surface model.

1640
01:10:46,175 --> 01:10:48,512
-That's ones small step
for special effects,

1641
01:10:48,545 --> 01:10:51,014
one giant leap
for our imagination.

1642
01:10:53,783 --> 01:10:55,285
-That was great.

1643
01:10:55,319 --> 01:10:57,220
There was nothing fake
about that one.

1644
01:11:04,594 --> 01:11:07,631
-Oh, these shorts are binding.

1645
01:11:07,664 --> 01:11:10,467
-Like his contract.

1646
01:11:10,500 --> 01:11:12,736
-Briteis, get your shoes on,
honey.

1647
01:11:12,769 --> 01:11:14,070
We're at grandpa's.

1648
01:11:14,103 --> 01:11:17,641
He might even have a quarter
for you, honey.

1649
01:11:17,674 --> 01:11:21,110
-We made it, didn't we?

1650
01:11:21,144 --> 01:11:23,279
-Excuse me, space food,
dehydrated ice cream, you know.

1651
01:11:23,313 --> 01:11:26,916
-Are we still air-tight?
-I guess so.

1652
01:11:26,950 --> 01:11:28,352
The pressure is still up.

1653
01:11:28,385 --> 01:11:33,357
[Beeping]

1654
01:11:33,390 --> 01:11:38,495
-Care to tango?
-Bill, I muffed it.

1655
01:11:38,528 --> 01:11:39,796
What'll we do?

1656
01:11:39,829 --> 01:11:41,130
-Take it easy.

1657
01:11:41,164 --> 01:11:44,334
Come on.
-But you don't understand.

1658
01:11:44,368 --> 01:11:45,669
There isn't enough fuel left.

1659
01:11:45,702 --> 01:11:47,971
We can't take off.

1660
01:11:48,004 --> 01:11:49,739
-Whew.

1661
01:11:49,773 --> 01:11:52,742
-Sorry to have gone female
on you, Major.

1662
01:11:52,776 --> 01:11:55,412
I didn't mean
to take advantage of you.

1663
01:11:55,445 --> 01:11:57,080
-That's all right.

1664
01:11:57,113 --> 01:12:00,417
I'm feeling kind of
punchy myself.

1665
01:12:00,450 --> 01:12:01,851
-What do you think
we should do?

1666
01:12:01,885 --> 01:12:05,321
-You're asking my advice,
officially?

1667
01:12:05,355 --> 01:12:06,956
-Yes, officially.
-Let's start a life.

1668
01:12:06,990 --> 01:12:08,758
You and me and the devil
makes three.

1669
01:12:08,792 --> 01:12:11,761
-Well, in that case, I, uh,

1670
01:12:11,795 --> 01:12:13,397
I think you'd better
powder your nose.

1671
01:12:13,430 --> 01:12:15,599
-Oh, go soak your head.

1672
01:12:15,632 --> 01:12:17,901
-And while you're doing that,
I'm going to get rid of him.

1673
01:12:17,934 --> 01:12:19,068
-What are you gonna do with him?

1674
01:12:19,102 --> 01:12:20,404
-Well, thought
I'd dress him like us.

1675
01:12:20,437 --> 01:12:21,971
That's pretty degrading.

1676
01:12:22,005 --> 01:12:25,509
-Well, I, uh, I could shove him
through the air lock,

1677
01:12:25,542 --> 01:12:28,344
but instead, I think I'll tie
him up in the store room.

1678
01:12:28,378 --> 01:12:29,779
I've got an idea about him
for later on.

1679
01:12:29,813 --> 01:12:31,415
-Yeah.
-What is it?

1680
01:12:31,448 --> 01:12:34,751
-Don't worry.
It'll be rough.

1681
01:12:34,784 --> 01:12:36,653
-Ooh, that one set off
the fire alarm.

1682
01:12:36,686 --> 01:12:38,922
-Now, we got more important
things to worry about,

1683
01:12:38,955 --> 01:12:40,857
how much food there is,
how much water, how much air...

1684
01:12:40,890 --> 01:12:42,091
-How much love.

1685
01:12:42,125 --> 01:12:43,126
-...and which one
runs out first.

1686
01:12:43,159 --> 01:12:44,828
-Oh, Bill.
They'll rescue us.

1687
01:12:44,861 --> 01:12:46,663
Certainly, they will.

1688
01:12:46,696 --> 01:12:49,633
-How?
How will they find us?

1689
01:12:49,666 --> 01:12:51,067
We've been out
of radio communication

1690
01:12:51,100 --> 01:12:53,737
ever since we went
around behind the Moon.

1691
01:12:53,770 --> 01:12:56,239
-Should've left a trail
of bread crumbs.

1692
01:12:56,272 --> 01:12:59,676
-We probably landed
out of sight of Earth.

1693
01:12:59,709 --> 01:13:01,611
-Might as well be on Neptune.
-Come on, little fella,

1694
01:13:01,645 --> 01:13:03,346
or you're gonna get bed sores.

1695
01:13:03,379 --> 01:13:06,015
-Slide, please.

1696
01:13:06,049 --> 01:13:07,984
Uh, this is us
at the Grand Canyon.

1697
01:13:08,017 --> 01:13:10,319
We got sore on those burros.

1698
01:13:10,353 --> 01:13:12,456
This is Glacier National Park

1699
01:13:12,489 --> 01:13:13,823
where little Bobby
turned his ankle.

1700
01:13:13,857 --> 01:13:17,494
We had to go back down early.

1701
01:13:17,527 --> 01:13:19,463
This is Space Mountain
at Disney World.

1702
01:13:19,496 --> 01:13:22,832
You know, Bobby lost his
corn dog over on the Matterhorn.

1703
01:13:22,866 --> 01:13:24,067
Am I boring you with this stuff?

1704
01:13:24,100 --> 01:13:25,469
-No, not at all.
-Oh.

1705
01:13:25,502 --> 01:13:35,311
**

1706
01:13:35,344 --> 01:13:37,313
Hey, look, a walk-in trash can.

1707
01:13:37,346 --> 01:13:38,848
-What a way to go.

1708
01:13:38,882 --> 01:13:41,851
**

1709
01:13:41,885 --> 01:13:43,820
-Bill, what can we do?

1710
01:13:43,853 --> 01:13:45,154
-First, let's find out
where we are.

1711
01:13:45,188 --> 01:13:47,123
-All right.
I'll take the Colestat,

1712
01:13:47,156 --> 01:13:48,291
and as soon as I have a fix,

1713
01:13:48,324 --> 01:13:49,959
you can feed tape
into the computer.

1714
01:13:49,993 --> 01:13:54,097
-Okay.
-Mm-hmm.

1715
01:13:57,867 --> 01:14:00,804
-Meanwhile, back
at Frisbee four.

1716
01:14:00,837 --> 01:14:02,105
-What's a Frisbee for?

1717
01:14:02,138 --> 01:14:03,773
-A report from the Magellan,
sir.

1718
01:14:03,807 --> 01:14:08,011
She is now exactly 18 minutes,
14 seconds overdue.

1719
01:14:08,044 --> 01:14:10,413
-That major must have
done something.

1720
01:14:10,446 --> 01:14:11,981
Why, they couldn't be even
40 seconds overdue

1721
01:14:12,015 --> 01:14:13,416
if they were in their orbit.

1722
01:14:13,449 --> 01:14:15,685
They've been interfered
with in some way or another.

1723
01:14:15,719 --> 01:14:17,286
Order a continuous
radar surveillance

1724
01:14:17,320 --> 01:14:19,255
of all their area
in their emerging sector

1725
01:14:19,288 --> 01:14:21,825
and keep an operator on that
radio until we hear from them.

1726
01:14:21,858 --> 01:14:23,660
-I'll stay here
and bark out orders.

1727
01:14:23,693 --> 01:14:29,766
**

1728
01:14:29,799 --> 01:14:32,435
-Where are we located?

1729
01:14:32,468 --> 01:14:35,572
-Well, according
to my calculations,

1730
01:14:35,605 --> 01:14:38,407
we're about 125 miles
past the crater Grimaldi...

1731
01:14:38,441 --> 01:14:40,309
-We're centrally located
just up the Slauson Cutoff.

1732
01:14:40,343 --> 01:14:42,111
-...approximately 11 miles
from the furthest position

1733
01:14:42,145 --> 01:14:44,848
in which we could beam a signal
to the satellite station.

1734
01:14:44,881 --> 01:14:47,016
-And two doors down from
the Subway sandwich shop.

1735
01:14:47,050 --> 01:14:49,018
-Bill, could you possibility
rig a relay for us

1736
01:14:49,052 --> 01:14:52,421
on one of those mountain peaks
in that direction?

1737
01:14:52,455 --> 01:14:54,423
-I don't know.

1738
01:14:54,457 --> 01:14:56,059
Our oxygen tanks
on our space suits

1739
01:14:56,092 --> 01:14:58,161
only carry a 4-hour supply.

1740
01:14:58,194 --> 01:15:03,099
-We could do a show.
My dad has a barn.

1741
01:15:03,132 --> 01:15:07,937
-...so it wouldn't be too
difficult if I can make it.

1742
01:15:07,971 --> 01:15:09,338
-Oh, Bill, you must.

1743
01:15:09,372 --> 01:15:11,841
You must!

1744
01:15:11,875 --> 01:15:14,644
-There's only one way
I can do it.

1745
01:15:14,678 --> 01:15:16,613
I'll have to go down and get
Wernher and take him with me.

1746
01:15:16,646 --> 01:15:21,017
**

1747
01:15:21,050 --> 01:15:22,652
-TV sets.
-Tons of them.

1748
01:15:22,686 --> 01:15:23,987
-Wow.
-Wernher, get up.

1749
01:15:26,956 --> 01:15:29,092
We've landed on the Moon.

1750
01:15:29,125 --> 01:15:30,594
Since we're beyond radio reach
of the Earth,

1751
01:15:30,627 --> 01:15:32,328
I have to go out
and set up a relay.

1752
01:15:32,361 --> 01:15:34,731
-Have fun.
-And you're coming with me.

1753
01:15:34,764 --> 01:15:36,232
Our lives depend upon us
doing this...

1754
01:15:36,265 --> 01:15:37,901
-Set up a relay with two guys,
it doesn't make sense.

1755
01:15:37,934 --> 01:15:39,736
It's not worth it.

1756
01:15:39,769 --> 01:15:41,270
-It's a gamble whether or not
we'll ever make it.

1757
01:15:41,304 --> 01:15:43,006
-Bet you a C-note we die.

1758
01:15:43,039 --> 01:15:45,441
-All right.
I'm with you.

1759
01:15:45,474 --> 01:15:48,077
I wouldn't have done this
if I hadn't been forced into it.

1760
01:15:48,111 --> 01:15:50,313
Let's go.
-Now, remember, one false move,

1761
01:15:50,346 --> 01:15:51,981
and you get this.
-Wow.

1762
01:15:52,015 --> 01:15:53,917
Cool.
Can I keep it?

1763
01:15:53,950 --> 01:15:55,184
-Well, your wrists
didn't grow together.

1764
01:15:55,218 --> 01:15:56,953
I was wrong.

1765
01:15:56,986 --> 01:16:04,393
**

1766
01:16:04,427 --> 01:16:07,631
-Uh, uh, this is the way
the first Wallenda died.

1767
01:16:07,664 --> 01:16:15,605
**

1768
01:16:15,639 --> 01:16:17,607
-Bill is sporting
a light cotton

1769
01:16:17,641 --> 01:16:19,242
jumper pleated at the knees

1770
01:16:19,275 --> 01:16:21,678
and is carrying a set
of rabbit ears.

1771
01:16:24,347 --> 01:16:26,515
-Notice the rich styling
on his rabbit ears.

1772
01:16:26,549 --> 01:16:28,618
Very futuristic.

1773
01:16:28,652 --> 01:16:34,490
While the turncoat --
while the turncoat, thank you,

1774
01:16:34,523 --> 01:16:36,492
enjoys a spandex sport suit

1775
01:16:36,525 --> 01:16:40,764
fashionable for either work,
play, or sabotage.

1776
01:16:40,797 --> 01:16:44,500
-From Moonflex, $150.

1777
01:16:44,533 --> 01:16:45,601
-Hmm.

1778
01:16:45,635 --> 01:16:52,942
**

1779
01:16:52,976 --> 01:17:00,349
**

1780
01:17:00,383 --> 01:17:02,085
-Wish us luck, Colonel.

1781
01:17:02,118 --> 01:17:04,353
-Good luck, Bill.
Happy landing.

1782
01:17:07,691 --> 01:17:10,626
-Didn't they already land?

1783
01:17:10,660 --> 01:17:12,461
-Suckers.

1784
01:17:12,495 --> 01:17:13,596
-[Laughs]

1785
01:17:13,629 --> 01:17:22,906
**

1786
01:17:22,939 --> 01:17:24,173
-Bill, do you read me?

1787
01:17:24,207 --> 01:17:25,441
Come in, Bill.
-Wow.

1788
01:17:25,474 --> 01:17:27,076
That's where my old school
used to be.

1789
01:17:27,110 --> 01:17:29,345
You know, all those hills
weren't there before.

1790
01:17:29,378 --> 01:17:31,447
-I'll call you back
in about 2 hours.

1791
01:17:31,480 --> 01:17:33,082
-Okay, but Mom
is calling later.

1792
01:17:33,116 --> 01:17:36,152
So if it's busy, keep trying.

1793
01:17:36,185 --> 01:17:38,021
-They look like salt
and pepper shakers.

1794
01:17:41,825 --> 01:17:43,492
-Hey, uh, Bub,
I'll give you a nickel

1795
01:17:43,526 --> 01:17:46,796
if you scale that cliff,
and I'll show you my sore toe.

1796
01:17:49,699 --> 01:17:51,634
-Cliff diving on the Moon.

1797
01:17:54,838 --> 01:17:56,973
-Their heads look
like bell jars.

1798
01:17:57,006 --> 01:17:59,008
-Come on. Be a man.
Get up that rock.

1799
01:17:59,042 --> 01:18:00,243
You're one-sixth
your own weight.

1800
01:18:00,276 --> 01:18:01,477
Come on, you wuss.

1801
01:18:01,510 --> 01:18:03,112
Beat cheeks.

1802
01:18:03,146 --> 01:18:06,249
I can believe what a spaz
you are, now come on.

1803
01:18:06,282 --> 01:18:08,985
If you don't get your butt up on
that rock on the count of three,

1804
01:18:09,018 --> 01:18:10,286
I'm going to hurt
your insides, boy.

1805
01:18:10,319 --> 01:18:11,654
That's right.

1806
01:18:11,687 --> 01:18:17,493
**

1807
01:18:17,526 --> 01:18:19,562
-Night of a thousand stars.

1808
01:18:19,595 --> 01:18:28,471
**

1809
01:18:28,504 --> 01:18:30,439
-Wow.
That ship is only 3 feet away.

1810
01:18:30,473 --> 01:18:32,608
It looks like a model from here.

1811
01:18:32,641 --> 01:18:33,943
Well, it is.

1812
01:18:38,281 --> 01:18:40,449
-Well, now, at least
we'll be able to get HBO.

1813
01:18:44,720 --> 01:18:47,490
-Briteis, we have established
the relay station,

1814
01:18:47,523 --> 01:18:49,525
and we're returning
to the ship now.

1815
01:18:49,558 --> 01:18:53,129
Our oxygen reserve may not
be enough to carry us back.

1816
01:18:53,162 --> 01:18:57,834
If, uh, if we don't make it,
good luck and God bless you.

1817
01:18:57,867 --> 01:18:59,068
-I'm sorry, uh, what?

1818
01:18:59,102 --> 01:19:00,569
I wasn't listening.

1819
01:19:00,603 --> 01:19:04,407
**

1820
01:19:04,440 --> 01:19:06,275
-Be very careful going down.

1821
01:19:06,309 --> 01:19:09,312
Those rocks can be
very, very slippery.

1822
01:19:09,345 --> 01:19:11,147
-Oh, come on.
What could happen?

1823
01:19:11,180 --> 01:19:12,849
We're one-sixth our own weight.

1824
01:19:12,882 --> 01:19:15,251
Nothing could -- Oh!

1825
01:19:15,284 --> 01:19:19,022
Ohhh!

1826
01:19:19,055 --> 01:19:20,423
Ugh! Right in the butt.

1827
01:19:20,456 --> 01:19:22,826
-Hey, quit fooling
around down there.

1828
01:19:22,859 --> 01:19:25,328
-Nice half-gainer.

1829
01:19:25,361 --> 01:19:27,330
-Oh, rats.
Now, I got to drag him back.

1830
01:19:27,363 --> 01:19:30,967
-I'm half traitor now.
-Half traitor?

1831
01:19:31,000 --> 01:19:33,002
-Bill!
Come in, Bill!

1832
01:19:33,036 --> 01:19:41,344
**

1833
01:19:41,377 --> 01:19:44,848
-What?
Sleeping on the job again, huh?

1834
01:19:44,881 --> 01:19:48,484
-Looks like we got another
fish tank for the ship.

1835
01:19:48,517 --> 01:19:52,155
Oh, I hate when that happens,
poor guy.

1836
01:19:52,188 --> 01:19:57,726
**

1837
01:19:57,760 --> 01:20:03,566
**

1838
01:20:03,599 --> 01:20:06,669
-By this time, my lungs
were aching for air.

1839
01:20:06,702 --> 01:20:08,404
-Isn't that a line
from "Sea Hunt"?

1840
01:20:08,437 --> 01:20:10,439
-Yeah.
-You must!

1841
01:20:10,473 --> 01:20:13,977
-Get back to where you once
belonged, Jojo.

1842
01:20:14,010 --> 01:20:15,744
-Well, I didn't mean
to hurt anybody.

1843
01:20:15,778 --> 01:20:16,846
We were just playing.

1844
01:20:16,880 --> 01:20:18,347
Mom says sometimes
I play too rough.

1845
01:20:18,381 --> 01:20:20,449
I'm probably a lot bigger
than the other kids are,

1846
01:20:20,483 --> 01:20:22,485
and, well,

1847
01:20:22,518 --> 01:20:25,021
I was just goofing off,
and now, I guess he's dead.

1848
01:20:25,054 --> 01:20:26,990
I was playing, and it wasn't
really my fault.

1849
01:20:27,023 --> 01:20:28,357
I feel like such a jerk.

1850
01:20:28,391 --> 01:20:30,126
I never really killed
anybody before.

1851
01:20:30,159 --> 01:20:32,395
Geez.
Oh, I don't know.

1852
01:20:32,428 --> 01:20:34,597
I just got to learn to be around
with the other kids.

1853
01:20:34,630 --> 01:20:37,133
I spend a lot of time
with my books in my room and --

1854
01:20:37,166 --> 01:20:40,803
Oh, I don't know.
I'm just no good.

1855
01:20:40,836 --> 01:20:44,173
I-I don't even deserve
to eat supper tonight.

1856
01:20:44,207 --> 01:20:45,741
-Briteis.

1857
01:20:45,774 --> 01:20:48,177
I was just barely able
to make it.

1858
01:20:52,215 --> 01:20:54,150
-Hey, this is no time
to make dust angels.

1859
01:20:54,183 --> 01:20:57,653
Get up!
-Let --

1860
01:20:57,686 --> 01:20:59,388
Let down the hoist bar.

1861
01:21:01,891 --> 01:21:03,259
-Hey, pretend your legs
don't work.

1862
01:21:03,292 --> 01:21:04,627
That's cool.
Yeah.

1863
01:21:04,660 --> 01:21:08,331
That's fun.

1864
01:21:08,364 --> 01:21:13,769
-Briteis.
I'm so cold.

1865
01:21:13,802 --> 01:21:16,906
I'm so cold.

1866
01:21:16,940 --> 01:21:18,107
-Well, you're not that bad
of a guy.

1867
01:21:18,141 --> 01:21:20,176
you've got your warm moments.

1868
01:21:20,209 --> 01:21:26,849
**

1869
01:21:26,882 --> 01:21:31,454
-[Humming]

1870
01:21:31,487 --> 01:21:36,325
-[Whistling]

1871
01:21:36,359 --> 01:21:40,063
-[Humming circus music]

1872
01:21:40,096 --> 01:21:42,999
In the center ring for your
pleasure and excitement,

1873
01:21:43,032 --> 01:21:44,733
the only aerial act
on the Moon.

1874
01:21:44,767 --> 01:21:47,803
Come one, come all!

1875
01:21:47,836 --> 01:21:49,705
-It's a G.I. Joe action set.

1876
01:21:51,941 --> 01:21:55,144
Comes with Moon Man, Space Pod,
and detachable retinas.

1877
01:21:55,178 --> 01:21:58,281
-Head and legs sold separately.

1878
01:21:58,314 --> 01:22:01,985
-By Blamo.
-Magellan to SPACOM.

1879
01:22:02,018 --> 01:22:03,619
Magellan to SPACOM.

1880
01:22:03,652 --> 01:22:05,955
Come in, please.

1881
01:22:05,989 --> 01:22:09,425
-WMOON TV has canceled
its broadcast day.

1882
01:22:09,458 --> 01:22:11,294
We will resume programming
at 7:00 a.m.

1883
01:22:11,327 --> 01:22:14,430
And now, our national anthem.

1884
01:22:14,463 --> 01:22:15,999
-Magellan to SPACOM.

1885
01:22:16,032 --> 01:22:17,600
Magellan to SPACOM.

1886
01:22:17,633 --> 01:22:19,969
Come in, please.

1887
01:22:20,003 --> 01:22:23,839
-Could you make it work?
-I'm afraid not, Bill.

1888
01:22:23,872 --> 01:22:26,775
I've been trying for 5 hours,
and nothing's happened.

1889
01:22:26,809 --> 01:22:29,078
-Maybe we should've pointed
that antennae towards Earth.

1890
01:22:29,112 --> 01:22:31,314
-...switch isn't on?

1891
01:22:31,347 --> 01:22:34,150
-Should it be?
-Yes.

1892
01:22:34,183 --> 01:22:36,019
I hooked our emergency relay
to tie in

1893
01:22:36,052 --> 01:22:38,821
with the poor
television monitor.

1894
01:22:38,854 --> 01:22:41,157
Press number three
on your board.

1895
01:22:41,190 --> 01:22:43,726
-No, not that channel three.
-That's right.

1896
01:22:43,759 --> 01:22:47,463
-SPACOM to Magellan.
We read you.

1897
01:22:47,496 --> 01:22:49,665
-I should've came to
consciousness a lot sooner.

1898
01:22:49,698 --> 01:22:52,435
It would've saved us
a lot of trouble.

1899
01:22:52,468 --> 01:22:56,572
-Good.
SPACOM to Magellan.

1900
01:22:56,605 --> 01:22:58,074
-Took you so long to reach him
from the other room.

1901
01:22:58,107 --> 01:23:00,109
-Colonel Briteis,
will you please

1902
01:23:00,143 --> 01:23:01,277
get Dr. Wernher
out of the cabin?

1903
01:23:01,310 --> 01:23:02,845
I have a classified message
for you.

1904
01:23:02,878 --> 01:23:05,248
Scramble code Y, couple three.

1905
01:23:05,281 --> 01:23:07,616
-He's not in the cabin, sir.

1906
01:23:07,650 --> 01:23:09,152
-Are you sure that
he can't hear me?

1907
01:23:09,185 --> 01:23:11,587
-Quite sure, sir.
-Colonel, listen carefully.

1908
01:23:11,620 --> 01:23:13,689
Make him a prisoner at once.

1909
01:23:13,722 --> 01:23:15,324
Take him alive if you can,
but don't take any chances.

1910
01:23:15,358 --> 01:23:17,026
-It's a little too late
on that alive thing, sir.

1911
01:23:17,060 --> 01:23:18,827
-He isn't Dr. Wernher.
He's a spy.

1912
01:23:18,861 --> 01:23:20,763
He plans to take control
of the Magellan and crash

1913
01:23:20,796 --> 01:23:22,098
it into the space station.

1914
01:23:22,131 --> 01:23:24,633
The FBI have just rescued
the real Dr. Wernher.

1915
01:23:24,667 --> 01:23:26,502
-Yes, sir. We know.
-Repeat?

1916
01:23:26,535 --> 01:23:28,437
-Dr. Wernher is dead.

1917
01:23:28,471 --> 01:23:30,573
You see, he did try to take
over the ship,

1918
01:23:30,606 --> 01:23:32,275
and Major Moore stopped him,

1919
01:23:32,308 --> 01:23:33,576
but in the struggle,
the jets were fired,

1920
01:23:33,609 --> 01:23:35,844
and I found it necessary
to land on the Moon.

1921
01:23:35,878 --> 01:23:37,346
-You what?

1922
01:23:37,380 --> 01:23:40,916
-Repeat!
You did what?

1923
01:23:40,949 --> 01:23:42,818
-We had to land on the Moon.

1924
01:23:42,851 --> 01:23:44,687
-D'oh!

1925
01:23:44,720 --> 01:23:47,123
-Commodore, get me channeled
to the pentagon immediately.

1926
01:23:47,156 --> 01:23:49,425
The Magellan has just landed
on the Moon.

1927
01:23:49,458 --> 01:23:52,661
Colonel, report your present
situation in detail.

1928
01:23:52,695 --> 01:23:55,931
-Yes, sir.
Landed on Luna approximately

1929
01:23:55,964 --> 01:23:58,834
125 miles
back of crater Grimaldi

1930
01:23:58,867 --> 01:24:01,637
and approximately 10 miles
beyond the terminator.

1931
01:24:01,670 --> 01:24:03,272
-There's a Howard Johnson's
at the interchange,

1932
01:24:03,306 --> 01:24:05,108
and we passed a Stuckey's.
-Anybody hurt?

1933
01:24:05,141 --> 01:24:07,410
-Only fatality, Dr. Wernher.

1934
01:24:07,443 --> 01:24:09,278
He died during
the establishment of the radio

1935
01:24:09,312 --> 01:24:11,580
relay 11 miles
from our present position.

1936
01:24:11,614 --> 01:24:12,915
This relay is the means to which

1937
01:24:12,948 --> 01:24:14,450
we've established
contact with you.

1938
01:24:14,483 --> 01:24:15,951
-Are you in any
immediate danger?

1939
01:24:15,984 --> 01:24:17,520
-No, sir.

1940
01:24:17,553 --> 01:24:19,522
-Well, the boredom is
getting pretty relentless.

1941
01:24:19,555 --> 01:24:23,692
-I'll, uh, I'll call you
in exactly 60 minutes.

1942
01:24:23,726 --> 01:24:28,030
**

1943
01:24:30,866 --> 01:24:34,437
**

1944
01:24:34,470 --> 01:24:36,905
-SPACOM to Magellan.
General Greene speaking.

1945
01:24:36,939 --> 01:24:38,807
-You know, you're wasting
your life in front of that TV.

1946
01:24:38,841 --> 01:24:40,075
-Magellan to SPACOM.

1947
01:24:40,109 --> 01:24:42,010
Yes, General?
Moore speaking.

1948
01:24:42,044 --> 01:24:44,012
Colonel Briteis
standing by, sir.

1949
01:24:44,046 --> 01:24:45,981
-Good.
Now pay attention, Bill.

1950
01:24:46,014 --> 01:24:47,850
Now that you two are
on the Moon, stay there.

1951
01:24:47,883 --> 01:24:48,917
Do you understand?
-That's easy.

1952
01:24:48,951 --> 01:24:50,153
-Yes, sir. But I --

1953
01:24:50,186 --> 01:24:51,654
-Pay attention
and don't interrupt.

1954
01:24:51,687 --> 01:24:53,589
The joint chiefs have conferred
with the President,

1955
01:24:53,622 --> 01:24:55,524
and it has been decided
to change your mission.

1956
01:24:55,558 --> 01:24:57,626
You are now Moon
Base Number One.

1957
01:24:57,660 --> 01:24:58,927
-Oh, great.
-You will remain where you are

1958
01:24:58,961 --> 01:25:00,763
and show the flag at such time

1959
01:25:00,796 --> 01:25:02,831
that you can be relieved by
a larger, permanent follow-up.

1960
01:25:02,865 --> 01:25:04,733
-Show the flag to who?
-In the meantime,

1961
01:25:04,767 --> 01:25:08,637
ration your water, food and air
to last at least 10 days.

1962
01:25:08,671 --> 01:25:11,407
That's within safe limits
based on what you have.

1963
01:25:11,440 --> 01:25:13,509
We'll undertake to get
more supplies over to you

1964
01:25:13,542 --> 01:25:15,144
in that time.

1965
01:25:15,178 --> 01:25:17,680
Colonel, you've had
drone pilot experience.

1966
01:25:17,713 --> 01:25:20,283
Bill, can you breadboard
your ship's radio equipment

1967
01:25:20,316 --> 01:25:23,018
to permit it to jockey
supply rockets down?

1968
01:25:23,051 --> 01:25:24,587
-Well, we don't have
anything better to do.

1969
01:25:24,620 --> 01:25:26,689
-I guess so, sir.
I better be able to.

1970
01:25:26,722 --> 01:25:29,258
-I'll have one of the electron-
pushing boys advise you on it.

1971
01:25:29,292 --> 01:25:30,959
I know it can be done.
They've already told me so.

1972
01:25:30,993 --> 01:25:32,428
-Oh, ho, la, di, da.
-That will be all now,

1973
01:25:32,461 --> 01:25:34,397
Moon Base Number One,
but don't go away, Bill.

1974
01:25:34,430 --> 01:25:38,667
Oh, Colonel, I have a classified
message for Major Moore only.

1975
01:25:38,701 --> 01:25:40,803
Would you be so kind as
to go aft and close the hatch?

1976
01:25:40,836 --> 01:25:42,171
-Huh?

1977
01:25:42,205 --> 01:25:45,341
-You heard what he said,
down below.

1978
01:25:45,374 --> 01:25:48,777
-Very well, sir, if you say so.
-She can just put her

1979
01:25:48,811 --> 01:25:49,945
hands over her ears and go,

1980
01:25:49,978 --> 01:25:51,814
"La, la, la, la, la,
la, la, la, la."

1981
01:25:51,847 --> 01:25:54,317
[Beeping]

1982
01:25:54,350 --> 01:25:56,685
-Oh, Bill, scramble code E,
combo two.

1983
01:25:56,719 --> 01:25:58,921
-Yes, sir.

1984
01:25:58,954 --> 01:26:02,090
Over and out on clear.

1985
01:26:02,124 --> 01:26:03,492
Standing by on scramble.

1986
01:26:03,526 --> 01:26:06,729
-Can you read me, Bill?
-Yes, sir.

1987
01:26:06,762 --> 01:26:08,397
-Take off your rank, son.

1988
01:26:08,431 --> 01:26:12,501
This is man to man.
-Mano a mano.

1989
01:26:12,535 --> 01:26:13,936
-Spill it.

1990
01:26:13,969 --> 01:26:15,238
-How is it with you
and Briteis?

1991
01:26:15,271 --> 01:26:16,805
You still sore on her?

1992
01:26:16,839 --> 01:26:18,173
-No, uh...

1993
01:26:18,207 --> 01:26:21,277
-Good thing she went below,
you know?

1994
01:26:21,310 --> 01:26:22,945
-Seems to me you used
to be pretty

1995
01:26:22,978 --> 01:26:25,348
sweet on her way back when.
-Ah, nice kid.

1996
01:26:25,381 --> 01:26:27,450
Kind of nosy, though.
-Tell me the truth, Bill.

1997
01:26:27,483 --> 01:26:29,252
I've got to know.

1998
01:26:29,285 --> 01:26:31,587
Could it be that
you're still sweet on her?

1999
01:26:31,620 --> 01:26:34,957
Tell me the truth,
or don't answer at all.

2000
01:26:34,990 --> 01:26:36,158
-He's not Dr. Bellow.

2001
01:26:36,191 --> 01:26:38,727
He's Dr. Chuck Woolery.

2002
01:26:38,761 --> 01:26:40,095
-Matthew, what are
you driving at?

2003
01:26:40,128 --> 01:26:42,431
-No, no.
Don't get shirty, son.

2004
01:26:42,465 --> 01:26:43,966
I don't like prying
into your affairs

2005
01:26:43,999 --> 01:26:45,634
any more than you like it.

2006
01:26:45,668 --> 01:26:47,836
But I've just been talking
with the White House.

2007
01:26:47,870 --> 01:26:49,672
The president thinks,
and so do I.

2008
01:26:49,705 --> 01:26:52,808
-The president thinks?
That's something new.

2009
01:26:52,841 --> 01:26:54,477
-You're gonna be shut up
in that tin can

2010
01:26:54,510 --> 01:26:56,679
with a pretty young woman
for weeks, maybe months.

2011
01:26:56,712 --> 01:26:59,282
Public opinion being what it is,
it'd be a lot better

2012
01:26:59,315 --> 01:27:02,585
for everyone, for the country,
for the service,

2013
01:27:02,618 --> 01:27:05,754
and for you
if you two were married.

2014
01:27:05,788 --> 01:27:07,456
-What are you saying?
I'm no rabbit.

2015
01:27:07,490 --> 01:27:09,992
-Just think it over, boy.

2016
01:27:10,025 --> 01:27:13,161
I can't give you an order
on this, but, uh, think it over.

2017
01:27:15,798 --> 01:27:19,167
-Listen, Papi, she wouldn't
have me on a silver platter.

2018
01:27:19,201 --> 01:27:20,636
-How about in a tin can?
-Are you sure?

2019
01:27:20,669 --> 01:27:22,738
-How about an air mattress
slathered with butter?

2020
01:27:22,771 --> 01:27:24,907
-Maybe you haven't gone
about it right.

2021
01:27:24,940 --> 01:27:27,443
-Sorry.
-But you don't understand.

2022
01:27:27,476 --> 01:27:33,015
She, uh, she has no use for me.
-Have you tried asking her?

2023
01:27:33,048 --> 01:27:37,119
-Man, never thought of that.
-May I come up now?

2024
01:27:37,152 --> 01:27:39,755
It's lonely down here.
-Oh, sure.

2025
01:27:39,788 --> 01:27:42,090
I was just gonna tell you
that the General was through.

2026
01:27:42,124 --> 01:27:44,059
-And so are
my wild bachelor days.

2027
01:27:46,695 --> 01:27:48,464
-What did he say?

2028
01:27:48,497 --> 01:27:50,466
-Well, uh, oh...
-Say it.

2029
01:27:50,499 --> 01:27:51,567
-Ask her.
-Go on.

2030
01:27:51,600 --> 01:27:52,735
-Go on.
-...nothing.

2031
01:27:52,768 --> 01:27:54,770
-Come on, ask her.
-Don't be a pansy.

2032
01:27:54,803 --> 01:27:55,804
-Do it.
-Colonel Briteis?

2033
01:27:55,838 --> 01:27:57,272
-Yes, Bill?
-Ask her.

2034
01:27:57,306 --> 01:27:58,641
-Ask her.
-Say it.

2035
01:27:58,674 --> 01:28:00,743
-Go, go, go, go, go, go.

2036
01:28:00,776 --> 01:28:03,646
-I was wondering
if you could, uh...

2037
01:28:03,679 --> 01:28:06,148
-Could what, Bill?
-Ask her.

2038
01:28:06,181 --> 01:28:09,284
-Well, uh, I mean,
could you...

2039
01:28:09,318 --> 01:28:10,285
-Yes, yes?

2040
01:28:10,319 --> 01:28:13,021
-Oh, Jiminy.
-Oh, skip it.

2041
01:28:13,055 --> 01:28:15,624
-We'll take it up later.
-Ugh!

2042
01:28:15,658 --> 01:28:17,626
-No!
Bonehead.

2043
01:28:17,660 --> 01:28:24,500
**

2044
01:28:24,533 --> 01:28:31,440
**

2045
01:28:31,474 --> 01:28:32,975
-Oh, they must have to watch

2046
01:28:33,008 --> 01:28:35,243
marital-aid tapes
now or something.

2047
01:28:35,277 --> 01:28:43,352
**

2048
01:28:43,386 --> 01:28:44,687
-What is that?

2049
01:28:44,720 --> 01:28:47,490
-This looks like an early
version of "Pong."

2050
01:28:47,523 --> 01:28:50,493
[Beeping]

2051
01:28:50,526 --> 01:28:55,964
**

2052
01:28:55,998 --> 01:29:01,770
**

2053
01:29:01,804 --> 01:29:02,938
Kind of redundant, huh?

2054
01:29:02,971 --> 01:29:03,939
-Yeah.

2055
01:29:03,972 --> 01:29:05,941
[Beeping]

2056
01:29:05,974 --> 01:29:11,780
**

2057
01:29:11,814 --> 01:29:14,850
-Oh, that's no fun at all.

2058
01:29:14,883 --> 01:29:16,452
Oh, no fun at all.

2059
01:29:16,485 --> 01:29:17,920
Look.
It fell right over.

2060
01:29:17,953 --> 01:29:22,425
-Crashed, is more like it.
-Bill, it's landed.

2061
01:29:22,458 --> 01:29:23,626
-Yeah.

2062
01:29:23,659 --> 01:29:24,993
I'll go out and see
what's in it.

2063
01:29:25,027 --> 01:29:26,462
-It's the minister.

2064
01:29:26,495 --> 01:29:36,505
**

2065
01:29:36,772 --> 01:29:39,608
-Sort of like
an interstellar dunk tank.

2066
01:29:39,642 --> 01:29:44,012
-Oh, the Wells Fargo racket
is coming down the street.

2067
01:29:44,046 --> 01:29:46,148
-He's called for
the breaking fastball.

2068
01:29:46,181 --> 01:29:56,191
**

2069
01:29:56,459 --> 01:29:58,961
-I got a rocket full of stuff,
and I'm gonna go get it.

2070
01:29:58,994 --> 01:30:00,629
Yes, yes, yes.

2071
01:30:03,799 --> 01:30:05,734
-Over this way, come here.

2072
01:30:05,768 --> 01:30:07,936
Right down in here.
-Now, remember.

2073
01:30:07,970 --> 01:30:09,605
Never attempt to remove the bees

2074
01:30:09,638 --> 01:30:13,742
from the hive
without proper protective gear.

2075
01:30:13,776 --> 01:30:15,143
-Hey, it looks like a V-2.

2076
01:30:15,177 --> 01:30:17,480
-Yeah, he could have had
a V -- I'm sorry.

2077
01:30:17,513 --> 01:30:18,947
No.
-Thanks.

2078
01:30:18,981 --> 01:30:22,685
**

2079
01:30:22,718 --> 01:30:24,987
And it's filled with cat food!

2080
01:30:28,724 --> 01:30:33,161
This rocket was supposed to go
to the Cat-Women of the Moon!

2081
01:30:33,195 --> 01:30:35,230
-Well, let's see
what we got here --

2082
01:30:35,263 --> 01:30:38,000
one .45-caliber handgun,

2083
01:30:38,033 --> 01:30:41,737
four clips of ammunition,
$100 in gold coins,

2084
01:30:41,770 --> 01:30:46,141
two packages of chewing gum,
two issue prophylactic. Shoot.

2085
01:30:46,174 --> 01:30:51,079
A guy could have a pretty good
time in Vegas with this stuff.

2086
01:30:51,113 --> 01:30:52,615
-No butter?

2087
01:30:52,648 --> 01:30:58,621
**

2088
01:30:58,654 --> 01:31:04,960
**

2089
01:31:04,993 --> 01:31:06,995
-They all say, "Occupant."

2090
01:31:07,029 --> 01:31:08,664
-No expense was spared

2091
01:31:08,697 --> 01:31:11,366
in spraying
these cardboard boxes gold.

2092
01:31:15,203 --> 01:31:16,839
-Bills, bills, bills.

2093
01:31:16,872 --> 01:31:20,843
-Bill, did they send
everything we need?

2094
01:31:20,876 --> 01:31:24,747
-Briteis, it's Christmas!
-They're all bills.

2095
01:31:24,780 --> 01:31:26,582
Oh, wait. That's me.

2096
01:31:26,615 --> 01:31:31,687
**

2097
01:31:31,720 --> 01:31:37,325
-Lots and lots of SPACOM.
-[Squeaking]

2098
01:31:37,359 --> 01:31:39,161
Hey, it's a giant
Rubik's cube.

2099
01:31:41,429 --> 01:31:42,765
-I love the smell of SPACOM
in the morning.

2100
01:31:42,798 --> 01:31:46,602
It smells like chicory.

2101
01:31:46,635 --> 01:31:49,605
-If you can solve this puzzle,
you can get married.

2102
01:31:52,207 --> 01:31:56,178
And then try
to solve that puzzle.

2103
01:31:56,211 --> 01:31:58,847
-Moon base to SPACOM,
come in, please.

2104
01:31:58,881 --> 01:32:01,416
-SPACOM to lunar base.

2105
01:32:01,449 --> 01:32:03,986
-I want to talk to General
Greene on a secure channel.

2106
01:32:04,019 --> 01:32:06,622
Colonel Briteis speaking.
-Yes, ma'am.

2107
01:32:06,655 --> 01:32:08,456
Scramble code F, combo one.

2108
01:32:08,490 --> 01:32:11,426
-Adam and Eve on a raft.
-What is it, Briteis?

2109
01:32:11,459 --> 01:32:12,961
Any trouble?
-No, not at this moment.

2110
01:32:12,995 --> 01:32:14,897
-Only with the invitations,
but we got them out in time.

2111
01:32:14,930 --> 01:32:17,399
-But, you know, we promised
to do something.

2112
01:32:17,432 --> 01:32:20,669
-Yes, and a good thing, too.

2113
01:32:20,703 --> 01:32:22,337
What about it?

2114
01:32:22,370 --> 01:32:24,840
-Well, if you expect me
to go through with it,

2115
01:32:24,873 --> 01:32:26,441
there's something
you've got to do for me.

2116
01:32:26,474 --> 01:32:28,677
-No, I'm not gonna emcee
your wedding.

2117
01:32:28,711 --> 01:32:30,646
-Well, at this point,

2118
01:32:30,679 --> 01:32:35,117
you can just about write
your own ticket, if I can do it.

2119
01:32:35,150 --> 01:32:37,285
What is it, kid?

2120
01:32:37,319 --> 01:32:39,254
-Psst, psst, psst, psst.
-Well, it's this.

2121
01:32:39,287 --> 01:32:40,589
-Dearly beloved,
we are gathered

2122
01:32:40,623 --> 01:32:41,990
together here
in the sight of God...

2123
01:32:42,024 --> 01:32:43,425
-He's in rented shorts.
-...to join this man

2124
01:32:43,458 --> 01:32:44,627
and this woman
in the holy bonds of matrimony.

2125
01:32:44,660 --> 01:32:46,494
-And that's the T-shirt her mom
got married in.

2126
01:32:46,528 --> 01:32:48,597
-Do you take this woman to be
your lawfully wedded wife,

2127
01:32:48,631 --> 01:32:51,767
to have and to hold, to love
and to cherish in sickness

2128
01:32:51,800 --> 01:32:54,569
and in health,
till death do you part?

2129
01:32:54,603 --> 01:32:56,639
-I do.
-Do you take this man to be

2130
01:32:56,672 --> 01:32:58,907
your lawfully wedded husband,
to have and to hold,

2131
01:32:58,941 --> 01:33:01,744
to love, honor and obey
in sickness and in health,

2132
01:33:01,777 --> 01:33:03,078
till death do you part?

2133
01:33:03,111 --> 01:33:04,747
-Or until the oxygen
wears out.

2134
01:33:04,780 --> 01:33:06,682
-Who giveth this woman?

2135
01:33:06,715 --> 01:33:08,083
-I give this woman.

2136
01:33:08,116 --> 01:33:09,084
-You have no right
to give that woman.

2137
01:33:09,117 --> 01:33:10,418
-Take her left hand.

2138
01:33:10,452 --> 01:33:12,187
Place the ring upon her finger.

2139
01:33:12,220 --> 01:33:16,792
**

2140
01:33:16,825 --> 01:33:19,061
-Where'd he get the ring?
-By the power vested in me,

2141
01:33:19,094 --> 01:33:21,730
I pronounce you man and wife.

2142
01:33:21,764 --> 01:33:22,998
-Well, you're a notary public.

2143
01:33:25,533 --> 01:33:29,805
-Mm, Juicy Fruit!

2144
01:33:29,838 --> 01:33:31,439
-Don't go away, kids.

2145
01:33:31,473 --> 01:33:35,077
The President of the United
States wants to speak to you.

2146
01:33:35,110 --> 01:33:36,845
-Hmm, I wonder
what we'll have to say.

2147
01:33:36,879 --> 01:33:38,313
-Huh?
-Huh?

2148
01:33:38,346 --> 01:33:40,749
-Major Moore, can you hear me?
-No, no, no.

2149
01:33:40,783 --> 01:33:42,184
Put the President on,
not his wife.

2150
01:33:42,217 --> 01:33:43,518
-Yes, ma'am.
-I have a wedding present

2151
01:33:43,551 --> 01:33:46,288
for you from the people
of the United States.

2152
01:33:46,321 --> 01:33:49,291
On my recommendation...
-It's Dr. Ruth.

2153
01:33:49,324 --> 01:33:52,394
-Hello, you're on the air.
-Can't see.

2154
01:33:52,427 --> 01:33:53,395
-...to the rank
of Brigadier General...

2155
01:33:53,428 --> 01:33:56,631
-Now I can.
-...United States Space Force.

2156
01:33:56,665 --> 01:33:59,668
You will consider yourself
detached from USS Magellan.

2157
01:33:59,702 --> 01:34:01,103
-It's very healthy to be

2158
01:34:01,136 --> 01:34:03,438
in a capsule
together with your friends.

2159
01:34:03,471 --> 01:34:07,075
-...US Moon Base Number One,
to guard and protect it

2160
01:34:07,109 --> 01:34:09,444
for the benefit
of all the free peoples of...

2161
01:34:09,477 --> 01:34:11,146
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Speed it up.

2162
01:34:11,179 --> 01:34:12,915
We got some things
we want to get to.

2163
01:34:12,948 --> 01:34:14,683
-Yeah, we got lives.
-And a life to lead.

2164
01:34:14,717 --> 01:34:18,821
**

2165
01:34:18,854 --> 01:34:22,124
-Here, honey. I got you this
poison throwing star.

2166
01:34:22,157 --> 01:34:25,160
-You like your wedding
present, dear?

2167
01:34:25,193 --> 01:34:27,963
-I didn't get you anything.

2168
01:34:27,996 --> 01:34:29,664
-What?!
-Boo!

2169
01:34:29,698 --> 01:34:30,598
-That's it?
-Boo!

2170
01:34:30,632 --> 01:34:32,500
-Oh, wow.
-That was great.

2171
01:34:32,534 --> 01:34:35,570
-The worst!
-Oh, pathetic!

2172
01:34:35,603 --> 01:34:45,247
**

2173
01:34:45,280 --> 01:34:54,923
**

2174
01:34:54,957 --> 01:34:56,925
[Doors thud and creak]

2175
01:34:56,959 --> 01:34:59,795
[Doors whir]

2176
01:34:59,828 --> 01:35:01,663
[Doors thud and clank]

2177
01:35:01,696 --> 01:35:02,965
[Door slams]

2178
01:35:02,998 --> 01:35:05,100
-Oh, we finally got
through that one.

2179
01:35:05,133 --> 01:35:06,969
Hey, thanks, everybody,
for your cards and letters.

2180
01:35:07,002 --> 01:35:08,904
They really help
pull us through.

2181
01:35:08,937 --> 01:35:11,073
-Yeah. We thought the Lord
was gonna call Joel home

2182
01:35:11,106 --> 01:35:12,607
until we got this card.
-Yeah.

2183
01:35:12,640 --> 01:35:14,476
Cambot, could we show
a picture of this one?

2184
01:35:14,509 --> 01:35:15,944
Crow, you want to read
that one, buddy?

2185
01:35:15,978 --> 01:35:17,512
-Yeah.
It says, "Please send us

2186
01:35:17,545 --> 01:35:20,148
information
on the 'MST 3000' fan club.

2187
01:35:20,182 --> 01:35:22,117
We enjoy it very much.
Thanks.

2188
01:35:22,150 --> 01:35:24,086
Happy holidays
from the both of us."

2189
01:35:24,119 --> 01:35:25,487
Whoa!
Look at these Swedes!

2190
01:35:25,520 --> 01:35:26,688
Whoa!
-Yeah.

2191
01:35:26,721 --> 01:35:27,790
You know how they say
when people get married,

2192
01:35:27,823 --> 01:35:29,191
they start looking alike?

2193
01:35:29,224 --> 01:35:31,193
These two have been together
a long time.

2194
01:35:31,226 --> 01:35:33,728
-My turn, my turn!
-Okay, let her go,

2195
01:35:33,762 --> 01:35:34,897
uh, Mr. Tom Servo, there.

2196
01:35:34,930 --> 01:35:36,164
-Right you are, Slappy.

2197
01:35:36,198 --> 01:35:37,732
This one comes from
a Jeff Conrad

2198
01:35:37,766 --> 01:35:39,234
in Bloomington, Minnesota.

2199
01:35:39,267 --> 01:35:40,803
"Greetings. I've been
a long-time fan of

2200
01:35:40,836 --> 01:35:42,971
'Mystery Science Theater 3000.'
A group of friends

2201
01:35:43,005 --> 01:35:45,340
and I watch it
every week on videotape.

2202
01:35:45,373 --> 01:35:47,075
Hey, tell me.
Will the new shows be in color

2203
01:35:47,109 --> 01:35:49,812
or all in black and white?
Sincerely, Jeff Conrad."

2204
01:35:49,845 --> 01:35:53,748
-Well, and we got this last one
here from fabulous Hawaii.

2205
01:35:53,782 --> 01:35:54,850
-Ooh!
-Yep, and it says...

2206
01:35:54,883 --> 01:35:56,084
-Ahh!
-..."Dear sirs,

2207
01:35:56,118 --> 01:35:57,485
I'd like to join your fan club.

2208
01:35:57,519 --> 01:35:59,287
Please send me information,
membership cards,

2209
01:35:59,321 --> 01:36:01,356
large sums of money."
Very funny.

2210
01:36:01,389 --> 01:36:04,126
That's from Sam Litzinger
from Honolulu.

2211
01:36:04,159 --> 01:36:05,327
-Aloha.
-Yeah.

2212
01:36:05,360 --> 01:36:06,728
Tom Servo, why don't you read

2213
01:36:06,761 --> 01:36:09,064
that information
for the people on Earth?

2214
01:36:09,097 --> 01:36:10,232
-Put it up there, Cambot.
Give it to me

2215
01:36:10,265 --> 01:36:12,234
one time on the CG,
Helvetica bold.

2216
01:36:12,267 --> 01:36:13,468
Will ya? All right.

2217
01:36:13,501 --> 01:36:15,603
The "Mystery Science Theater
3000" fan club,

2218
01:36:15,637 --> 01:36:20,075
PO box 5325,
Hopkins, Minnesota, 55343.

